= Algebraic number theory
{wiki}

= Elliptic unit
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

Elliptic units are algebraic units constructed by evaluating suitably normalized elliptic functions at CM torsion points and taking products or norms into ray class fields. Their distribution relations make them norm compatible in CM division towers. They generate an <Euler system of elliptic units> and their local <Coleman power series for a CM formal group> have logarithmic derivatives encoding critical <Hecke L-function> values.

= Euler system of elliptic units
{c}
{parent=Elliptic unit}

Elliptic-unit distribution gives norm-compatible units indexed by auxiliary squarefree ray moduli, with norm factor $1-\operatorname{Frob}_{\mathfrak q}^{-1}$ at a newly added prime. Kolyvagin derivative operators turn these units into classes with controlled valuations, and suitable auxiliary primes chosen using <Chebotarev density theorem> bound class-group characteristic ideals. The construction supplies the difficult divisibility in the CM <Iwasawa main conjecture>.

= Hecke character
{c}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

= Grössencharacter
{c}
{synonym}

A Hecke character of a <number field> is a continuous character of its <idele class group>. Its finite conductor records the local unit subgroups on which it is trivial. As an ideal character it is defined on fractional ideals prime to that conductor, with a prescribed rule on principal ideals coming from its archimedean character. A <CM elliptic curve> yields an algebraic Hecke character with one CM embedding in its <infinity type of a Hecke character>.

= Hecke character of a CM elliptic curve
{c}
{parent=Hecke character}
{title2=$\psi_E:\mathbb A_F^\times/F^\times\to\mathbb C^\times$}

Suppose $F$ contains the CM field $K$ and all CM endomorphisms are defined over $F$. <CM reciprocity> compares the torus with its idele-transformed lattice and gives $a(s)\in K^\times$ for finite ideles. Then $\psi_E(s)=a(s_f)/N_{F/K}(s)_\infty$. On principal ideles, $a(b_f)=N_{F/K}(b)$, so the character descends to the <idele class group>. At a good prime its ideal value reduces to the <Frobenius isogeny>. If the original field omits $K$, construct the character over $FK$ and induce the corresponding representation down to the original field.

= Grössencharacter of the square-lattice elliptic curve
{c}
{parent=Hecke character of a CM elliptic curve}
{title2=$\psi_E(\mathfrak a)=\text{the primary generator of }\mathfrak a$}

For $E:y^2=x^3-x$ over $\mathbb Q(i)$, the <Hecke character> has conductor $(1+i)^3$ and ideal infinity type $(1,0)$. Its value on an ideal prime to $2$ is its unique <primary Gaussian integer> generator. Rational $(1+i)^3$-torsion fixes the unit ambiguity of the Frobenius value. At a split prime $\mathfrak p$, its value reduces to the degree-$p$ <Frobenius isogeny>; at an inert rational prime $p$, its value is $-p$, reducing to the $p^2$-power <Frobenius isogeny>.

= Infinity type of a Hecke character
{parent=Hecke character}

For an algebraic <Hecke character>, the character on archimedean ideles is a product of integral powers of embeddings and their complex conjugates. In the convention $\psi((a))=a$ for primary $a$ over an imaginary quadratic field, the ideal character has type $(1,0)$ and the idele character has archimedean component $z^{-1}$. Distinguishing these conventions avoids inverse errors in CM <Galois representations>.

= Kummer-Dedekind theorem
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{c}

The factorization of a defining polynomial modulo a prime, provided the index is coprime to that prime, determines the prime ideals, ramification indices and residue degrees in the corresponding ring of integers.

= Squarefree reduction excludes a prime from the power-basis index
{parent=Kummer-Dedekind theorem}
{title2=$\overline f\text{ squarefree}\implies p\nmid[\mathcal O_K:\mathbb Z[\alpha]]$}

For an <algebraic integer> $\alpha$ generating a <number field>, the <polynomial discriminant> of its <minimal polynomial> $f$ equals the <discriminant of elements of a number field> for its power basis. A squarefree reduction modulo $p$ has nonzero <resultant> with its derivative, so $p\nmid\operatorname{disc}(f)$. The <discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice>, $\operatorname{disc}(f)=[\mathcal O_K:\mathbb Z[\alpha]]^2d_K$, proves the assertion.

// Target: arithmetic.bigb

= Quotient-ring proof of the Kummer-Dedekind theorem
{parent=Kummer-Dedekind theorem}
{title2=$\mathcal O_K/p\mathcal O_K\simeq\mathbb F_p[X]/(\overline m)$}

For an integral generator $\alpha$ of a <number field>, let $A=\mathbb Z[\alpha]$ and suppose $p$ is coprime to $[\mathcal O_K:A]$. Localizing at $p$ makes $A$ and the <ring of integers> equal, so reduction gives the displayed <quotient ring>. If $\overline m=\prod g_i^{e_i}$ with distinct monic <irreducible polynomials>, the <Chinese remainder theorem> gives local factors $\mathbb F_p[X]/(g_i^{e_i})$. Their <residue fields> have degrees $\deg g_i$ and their <maximal ideals> have <nilpotence indices of an ideal> $e_i$. Localizing the <Dedekind domain> $\mathcal O_K$ at the corresponding <prime ideals> gives <discrete valuation rings>, where this same <nilpotence index of an ideal> is the <ramification index of a prime ideal>. Thus $(p)=\prod(p,g_i(\alpha))^{e_i}$ and each <residue degree> is $\deg g_i$.

= Leopoldt conjecture
{title2=$\delta_{F,p}=0$}
{c}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

The Leopoldt conjecture says that the diagonal image of the global units of a <number field> in its local $p$-adic unit groups has $\mathbb Z_p$-rank $r_1+r_2-1$. A defect would measure a loss of rank after $p$-adic completion. It is a theorem for abelian extensions of $\mathbb Q$.

= Leopoldt theorem for abelian number fields
{c}
{parent=Leopoldt conjecture}

The <Leopoldt conjecture> holds for every finite abelian extension of $\mathbb Q$. The proof uses linear independence of $p$-adic logarithms of algebraic numbers. In cyclotomic towers it supplies the ranks of the global-unit terms in the <class-field unit sequence>; it is not needed for the <unramified Iwasawa torsion theorem> in arbitrary towers.

= Iwasawa theory
{c}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

Iwasawa theory studies arithmetic objects in infinite towers of <number fields>, often <Zp-extensions>. Passing to inverse limits turns growing <ideal class groups>, unit groups and <Galois groups> into <modules> over an <Iwasawa algebra>. Their algebraic ranks and characteristic ideals organize the growth and relate it to <p-adic L-functions>.

= Main conjecture of Iwasawa theory
{parent=Iwasawa theory}
{wiki}

= Iwasawa main conjecture
{c}
{synonym}

The classical Iwasawa main conjecture, now a theorem over $\mathbb Q$, identifies characteristic ideals of appropriate cyclotomic <Iwasawa modules> with ideals generated by <p-adic L-function> power series. For a nontrivial even character in the p-ramified convention, $\operatorname{char}_\Lambda X_\chi=(g_\chi)$ with $g_\chi((1+p)^{1-s}-1)=L_p(\chi,s)$. The <Kummer reflection in Iwasawa theory> accounts for the reflected odd class-group convention.

= Characteristic-ideal bridge from local units to the main conjecture
{parent=Main conjecture of Iwasawa theory}
{title2=$\operatorname{char}(E/C)\operatorname{char}(X)=\operatorname{char}(U/C)\operatorname{char}(Y)$}

In the real cyclotomic tower, the <class-field unit sequence> gives $0\to E/C\to U/C\to X\to Y\to0$, where $X$ is the p-ramified <Iwasawa module> and $Y$ the unramified one. The modules are torsion over each one-variable character component. Splitting the four-term sequence into two <short exact sequences> proves the displayed identity by multiplicativity of <characteristic ideals>. The <Iwasawa theorem on local cyclotomic units> computes $\operatorname{char}(U/C)$ analytically. Thus the main conjecture for $X$ is equivalent to $\operatorname{char}(E/C)=\operatorname{char}(Y)$; local interpolation alone does not prove this remaining global equality.

= Iwasawa theorem on local cyclotomic units
{c}
{parent=Main conjecture of Iwasawa theory}
{title2=$U_\infty^1/C_\infty^1\simeq\mathbb Z_p[[G]]/(I(G)\zeta_p)$}

For the real p-power cyclotomic tower, take norm limits of local principal units and closed cyclotomic-unit subgroups. The corrected Coleman logarithm identifies $U_\infty^1$ with the <Iwasawa algebra> of $G=\mathbb Z_p^\times/\{\pm1\}$. On a <symmetric cyclotomic unit> it gives $([b]-[a])\zeta_p$. A topological generator generates the cyclotomic-unit limit and the <augmentation ideal>, yielding the quotient description. This local calculation does not alone identify the characteristic ideal of the global Galois module.

= One-variable main conjecture for CM elliptic curves
{parent=Main conjecture of Iwasawa theory}
{title2=$\operatorname{char}_\Lambda X_\chi=(\mathcal L_\chi)$}

At a good split prime, the one-prime CM division tower has a pro-$p$ <Galois group> isomorphic to $\mathbb Z_p$. Its prime-ramified <Iwasawa module> is torsion on each finite-character component. The main conjecture identifies its <characteristic ideal> with the corresponding elliptic-unit <p-adic L-function>. The <class-field unit sequence> and equality of characteristic ideals of global-units modulo elliptic-units and of class groups prove the identification. The cyclotomic Selmer formulation requires the appropriate CM twist and two-variable specialization; the one-prime tower is not the cyclotomic tower.

= Mazur-Wiles theorem
{c}
{parent=Main conjecture of Iwasawa theory}

The Mazur-Wiles theorem proves the classical <Iwasawa main conjecture> for abelian extensions of $\mathbb Q$. Its construction of class fields uses Galois representations associated with <modular forms>, congruences with Eisenstein series and the <Eisenstein ideal>.

= Iwasawa module
{c}
{parent=Iwasawa theory}

An Iwasawa module is a <module> over an <Iwasawa algebra>. In arithmetic towers it usually comes from a norm inverse limit of units or class groups, or from a <compact Galois module> for a maximal abelian pro-$p$ extension. Specify which extension is allowed to ramify: the <p-ramified Iwasawa module> and the <unramified Iwasawa module> have different ranks.

= p-ramified Iwasawa module
{c}
{parent=Iwasawa module}
{title2=$X=\operatorname{Gal}(M_\infty/F_\infty)$}

Let $M_\infty$ be the maximal abelian pro-$p$ extension of $F_\infty$ unramified away from primes over $p$. Its <Galois group> is the p-ramified Iwasawa module. In the cyclotomic tower of $\mathbb Q(\mu_p)$ for odd $p$, its <Iwasawa-module rank> is $(p-1)/2$. It is therefore quite different from the torsion <unramified Iwasawa module>.

= Class-field unit sequence
{parent=p-ramified Iwasawa module}
{title2=$0\to\overline E_\infty\to U_\infty\to X\to Y\to0$}

Passing to norm limits in <Artin reciprocity> gives an <exact sequence> connecting closures of global units, local pro-$p$ units at primes over $p$, the <p-ramified Iwasawa module> and the <unramified Iwasawa module>. It explains arithmetic <Iwasawa-module ranks> by comparing local and global unit ranks. Roots of unity and the precise splitting conventions must be retained when seeking exact integral statements.

= Coleman power series
{title2=$f_u(T)$}
{c}
{parent=Class-field unit sequence}

A Coleman power series encodes a norm-compatible sequence of local units in a cyclotomic tower by a single integral power series evaluated at $\zeta_{p^n}-1$. A logarithmic derivative and trace correction give a measure, relating the local images of <cyclotomic units> to <p-adic L-functions>.

= Coleman interpolation theorem for cyclotomic local units
{c}
{parent=Coleman power series}
{title2=$f_u(\zeta_n-1)=u_n,\quad Nf_u=f_u$}

For odd $p$ and a <norm-compatible sequence of local units> in $\mathbb Q_p(\mu_{p^{n+1}})$, there is a unique $f_u\in\mathbb Z_p[[T]]^\times$ interpolating all its entries, and the <Coleman norm operator> fixes it. To construct it, choose integral unit polynomials $F_j$ interpolating entry $u_j$, and take a convergent subsequence of $N^jF_{2j}$ in the compact $(p,T)$-adic unit space. The congruence $NF\equiv F\pmod p$ and <Coleman norm contraction> show that its evaluation at any fixed level tends to the desired entry. Uniqueness follows from the <Weierstrass preparation theorem>: a nonzero integral power series has only finitely many zeros in the open unit disk. The norm relation on evaluations then proves norm fixation. Principal units correspond to $f_u(0)\equiv1\pmod p$.

= Coleman unit-measure exact sequence
{c}
{parent=Coleman power series}
{title2=$0\to\mathbb Z_p(1)\to V_\infty\to\mathbb Z_p[[\mathbb Z_p^\times]]\to\mathbb Z_p(1)\to0$}

Let $V_\infty$ be the norm limit of local <principal units> in the $p$-power <cyclotomic field> tower, for odd $p$. The <Coleman power series> $f_u$ gives the <Frobenius-corrected logarithm> $p^{-1}\log(f_u^p/\phi f_u)$, whose inverse <Amice transform> is supported on $\mathbb Z_p^\times$. This is the middle map in the displayed <exact sequence>; the last map is the first moment $\mu\mapsto\int x\,d\mu$. Its kernel consists of norm-compatible $p$-power <roots of unity>. The logarithmic difference equation has one resonant degree, namely degree one in $\log(1+T)$, accounting for the last term. <Complex conjugation> acts by $-1$ on both copies of $\mathbb Z_p(1)$. Taking its even part therefore identifies the local principal-unit limit in the real tower with $\mathbb Z_p[[\mathbb Z_p^\times/\{\pm1\}]]$.

= Norm-compatible sequence of local units
{parent=Coleman power series}
{title2=$U_\infty=\varprojlim_n U_n$}

= Norm-coherent sequence of local units
{synonym}

= Norm-compatible sequences of local units
{synonym}

Such a sequence $u=(u_n)$ satisfies $N_{n/n-1}u_n=u_{n-1}$ at every successive layer. The <inverse limit> uses the <field norm>, rather than inclusion of <units> from the lower <field>. In a p-power cyclotomic tower a <Coleman power series> interpolates the sequence, and its <Coleman norm operator> fixes the interpolating series.

= Cyclotomic higher logarithmic derivative
{parent=Coleman power series}
{title2=$\delta_k(u)=\left.D^{k-1}(Df_u/f_u)\right|_{T=0},\quad D=(1+T)d/dT$}

= Higher logarithmic derivative
{synonym}

For a norm-compatible sequence of local units, its <Coleman power series> $f_u\in\mathbb Z_p[[T]]^\times$ defines these integral additive homomorphisms. In the formal coordinate $z=\log(1+T)$ they are the derivatives of $\log f_u(e^z-1)$; the constant term of the logarithm is irrelevant. A Galois element with cyclotomic value $a$ multiplies the kth derivative by $a^k$. This is the multiplicative-formal-group version of the <Coates–Wiles homomorphism>.

= Coleman norm operator
{c}
{parent=Coleman power series}
{title2=$\varphi(\mathcal Nf)=\prod_{\xi^p=1}f(\xi(1+T)-1)$}

The determinant norm for the finite free extension $R/\varphi(R)$ defines $\mathcal N$ on units. After adjoining the p-th <roots of unity>, the norm is the displayed product of conjugates. A <Coleman power series> of a norm-compatible unit sequence satisfies $\mathcal Nf=f$. For symmetric cyclotomic units, the product relation follows directly from $\prod_{\xi^p=1}(1-\xi^aX^a)=1-X^{ap}$ when $p\nmid a$.

= Coleman norm-fixed lift
{c}
{parent=Coleman norm operator}
{title2=$w(\overline f)=\lim_{n\to\infty}N^nf$}

Every <unit> $\overline f\in\mathbb F_p[[T]]^\times$ has a unique <Coleman norm operator>-fixed lift to $\mathbb Z_p[[T]]^\times$. Choose any lift $f$ and use $Nf\equiv f\pmod p$. The consecutive quotients of the iterates are $N^n(Nf/f)\in1+p^{n+1}R$ by <Coleman norm contraction>, so they converge coefficientwise uniformly in the <p-adic integers>. Their limit is norm-fixed by continuity. Two such lifts have norm-fixed quotient in $1+pR$ and therefore coincide. Uniqueness also makes the lifting map multiplicative and independent of the initial lift.

= Canonical Coleman decomposition of power-series units
{parent=Coleman norm-fixed lift}
{title2=$R^\times\simeq W\times(1+pR)$}

Let $W$ be the <subgroup> fixed by the <Coleman norm operator>. The <Coleman norm-fixed lift> gives the unique factorization $f=w(\overline f)v$, with $v\in1+pR$. Multiplication consequently gives the displayed <direct product of groups>, while reduction identifies $W$ with $\mathbb F_p[[T]]^\times$. This is a decomposition of <unit groups>, not an identification of a power-series <ring> with a product of groups.

= Coleman norm contraction
{c}
{parent=Coleman norm operator}
{title2=$N(1+p^kR)\subseteq1+p^{k+1}R$}

For odd $p$, the <Coleman norm operator> satisfies the displayed inclusion for every $k\geq1$. Write $M_h$ for multiplication by $h$ on the <finite free module> $R$ over $\phi(R)$. Its <trace> is $p\phi(S(h))$, where $S$ is the <Coleman trace operator>. In $\det(I+p^kM_h)$ the linear term is therefore divisible by $p^{k+1}$, and all higher terms are divisible by $p^{2k}$. <Congruence reflection for Frobenius substitution> gives the assertion. A norm-fixed element of $1+pR$ lies in $1+p^kR$ for every $k$ and hence equals $1$.

= Frobenius substitution on cyclotomic power series
{c}
{parent=Coleman power series}
{title2=$\varphi(f)(T)=f((1+T)^p-1)$}

= Frobenius substitutions on cyclotomic power series
{c}
{synonym}

On $R=\mathbb Z_p[[T]]$, this injective substitution reduces modulo $p$ to $f(T)\mapsto f(T^p)$. The ring is a free module of rank $p$ over $\varphi(R)$, with basis $1,(1+T),\ldots,(1+T)^{p-1}$. Prove this first modulo $p$, where the change of basis from $1,T,\ldots,T^{p-1}$ is triangular and invertible, and then lift coefficients successively modulo powers of $p$.

= Congruence reflection for Frobenius substitution
{parent=Frobenius substitution on cyclotomic power series}
{title2=$\phi^{-1}(p^kR)=p^kR$}

For $R=\mathbb Z_p[[T]]$ and $\phi(f)=f((1+T)^p-1)$, reduction modulo $p$ is the injective substitution $\overline f(T)\mapsto\overline f(T^p)$. Thus $\phi(f)\in pR$ implies $f\in pR$. Dividing by $p$ and repeating proves the displayed equality for every $k\geq1$. In particular, a <ring congruence> of <Frobenius substitutions on cyclotomic power series> reflects back to the original <formal power series>.

= Coleman trace operator
{c}
{parent=Frobenius substitution on cyclotomic power series}
{title2=$\varphi\psi(f)=p^{-1}\sum_{\xi^p=1}f(\xi(1+T)-1)$}

In the unique decomposition $f=\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}(1+T)^i\varphi(f_i)$, define $\psi(f)=f_0$. Averaging translates over the p-th <roots of unity> annihilates every nonzero basis index and retains the zeroth, proving the displayed identity and its integrality. Thus $\psi\varphi=1$ and $\psi(\varphi(g)f)=g\psi(f)$. Under the <Amice transform>, $\psi=0$ characterizes measures supported on $\mathbb Z_p^\times$.

= Coleman power series for a CM formal group
{c}
{parent=Coleman power series}
{title2=$f_u(t_n)=u_n$}

For a height-one <CM elliptic curve> formal group and compatible $\mathfrak p^n$-division parameters $t_n$, an integral Coleman series interpolates a norm-compatible sequence of local units $u_n$. Its norm relation is multiplication of its translates by the $\mathfrak p$-torsion, with coefficient Frobenius where required by the unramified base. <Coates–Wiles homomorphisms> and the <Frobenius-corrected logarithm> turn this local series into critical values and measures.

= Frobenius-corrected logarithm
{c}
{parent=Coleman power series for a CM formal group}
{title2=$\mathcal L_f(t)=\frac1p\log\frac{f(t)^p}{f([\pi]t)}$}

Assume $K_\mathfrak p=\mathbb Q_p$, $p>2$, $f(t)\in\mathbb Z_p[[t]]^\times$, and $[\pi]t\equiv t^p\pmod p$. Then the displayed ratio lies in $1+p\mathbb Z_p[[t]]$, so its logarithm divided by $p$ is integral. A formal-group isomorphism over the completed unramified coefficient ring transfers it to an integral <Amice transform>. A norm relation makes the corresponding <p-adic measure> supported on units. Its $k$th moment has the Euler correction $1-\pi^k/p$.

= Integral lifting of the corrected cyclotomic logarithm
{parent=Frobenius-corrected logarithm}
{title2=$g'(0)=0\iff g=\frac1p\log(f^p/\phi(f))$}

Let $R=\mathbb Z_p[[T]]$, $p$ odd, and $\phi(T)=(1+T)^p-1$. An element $g\in R$ is the <Frobenius-corrected logarithm> of a <unit> $f$ with $f(0)\in1+p\mathbb Z_p$ if and only if $g'(0)=0$. Necessity follows by differentiating at zero, using $\phi'(0)=p$. For sufficiency put $H=\exp(pg)\in1+pR$ and solve $f^p=H\phi(f)$. Choose $f_0=\exp(pg_0/(p-1))$. The linear equation reduces to $H_1=0$, so $f_1$ is free. In degree $n\geq2$ the unknown $f_n$ has coefficient $p f_0^{p-1}(1-p^{n-1})$. The remaining terms are divisible by $p$ because reduction of any integral truncated $f$ satisfies $f^p\equiv\phi(f)\pmod p$. Division gives a unique integral $f_n$. Two lifts differ by $(1+T)^c$, $c\in\mathbb Z_p$, as follows from the logarithmic equation in the coordinate $z=\log(1+T)$. If also $Sg=0$, the <Coleman trace operator> gives $\log(Nf/f)=pSg=0$. As $Nf/f\in1+pR$, injectivity of the <p-adic logarithm> implies $Nf=f$.

= Coates–Wiles homomorphism
{c}
{parent=Coleman power series for a CM formal group}
{title2=$\delta_k(u)=\left.\frac{d^k}{dz^k}\log f_u(\exp_{\widehat E}z)\right|_{z=0}$}

The $k$th logarithmic derivative in the additive formal logarithm coordinate defines the Coates–Wiles homomorphism. Products of local units become sums under this map. For <elliptic units>, differentiating the underlying complex elliptic function expresses the result in terms of critical <Hecke L-function> values divided by a <CM period>.

= Unramified Iwasawa module
{parent=Iwasawa module}
{title2=$Y=\operatorname{Gal}(L_\infty/F_\infty)$}

For a <Zp-extension>, let $L_\infty$ be the maximal unramified abelian pro-$p$ extension of $F_\infty$. Its <Galois group> is a compact <Iwasawa module>, identifiable by <Artin reciprocity> with the inverse limit of the $p$-primary <ideal class groups> of finite layers under norms.

= Unramified Iwasawa torsion theorem
{title2=$\operatorname{rank}_\Lambda Y=0$}
{parent=Unramified Iwasawa module}

The <unramified Iwasawa module> is finitely generated torsion over the <Iwasawa algebra of a Zp-extension>, for every <Zp-extension> of a <number field>. After a finite shift all ramified primes are totally ramified and their number $s$ is constant. <Class field theory> bounds finite-layer <coinvariant modules> by $\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb Z_p}Y_{\Gamma_n}\leq s-1$. The <compact Nakayama lemma> proves finite generation, and a positive <Iwasawa-module rank> would force ranks at least $r p^n$, a contradiction. No <Leopoldt conjecture> is required.

= Iwasawa module structure theorem
{c}
{parent=Iwasawa module}

A finitely generated <module> over $\Lambda=\mathbb Z_p[[T]]$ is <pseudo-isomorphic> to
$$
\Lambda^r\oplus\bigoplus_i\Lambda/(p^{a_i})\oplus\bigoplus_j\Lambda/(f_j^{b_j}),
$$
where the $f_j$ are irreducible <distinguished polynomials>. Height-one localizations are discrete valuation rings, which give the elementary divisors; the remaining errors are supported only at the maximal ideal and are finite. This is a finite-error classification, not necessarily an actual direct-sum decomposition.

= Euler characteristic of a one-variable Iwasawa module
{c}
{parent=Iwasawa module structure theorem}
{title2=$\chi_\Gamma(M)=\#M_\Gamma/\#M^\Gamma=|f_M(0)|_p^{-1}$}

Assume the invariant and coinvariant groups are finite and $f_M$ generates the <characteristic ideal> over $\mathbb Z_p[[S]]$, $S=\gamma-1$. The structure theorem and multiplicativity of finite Euler characteristics prove the formula; finite module errors have Euler characteristic one. If $f_M\mid f_N$ and the two Euler characteristics agree, the quotient has p-adic-unit constant term and is therefore a unit power series. Thus divisibility plus this finite arithmetic comparison upgrades to equality of characteristic ideals.

= Characteristic ideal
{parent=Iwasawa module structure theorem}
{title2=$\operatorname{char}_\Lambda M$}

For a finitely generated torsion <Iwasawa module>, its characteristic ideal is the product of the height-one prime ideals raised to the lengths of the corresponding localizations. In the elementary-divisor description it is generated by $p^{\sum a_i}\prod f_j^{b_j}$. It is unchanged by finite modules and multiplicative in <short exact sequences> of torsion modules.

= Characteristic series of an Iwasawa module
{parent=Characteristic ideal}
{title2=$\operatorname{char}_{\mathbb Z_p[[S]]}(M)=(f_M(S))$}

= Characteristic series
{synonym}

= Characteristic power series
{synonym}

For a finitely generated torsion <Iwasawa module>, this is a generator of its <characteristic ideal>, unique up to a <unit> of the <formal power series> ring. It may be chosen as a power of $p$ times a product of distinguished polynomials. The series itself and the ideal it generates are different objects; changing a generator by a <unit> preserves the ideal. When the finite <Euler characteristic of a one-variable Iwasawa module> is defined, the inverse p-adic absolute value of its constant term gives that invariant.

= Iwasawa invariants
{c}
{parent=Characteristic ideal}
{title2=$\mu(M)=\sum_i a_i,\quad\lambda(M)=\sum_j b_j\deg f_j$}

The invariants $\mu,\lambda$ of a finitely generated torsion <Iwasawa module> measure its $p$-power elementary factors and distinguished-polynomial factors. Vanishing of $\mu$ means that the module is finitely generated over $\mathbb Z_p$, up to finite error. It does not mean that the module is finite: a factor $\Lambda/(T)$ has $\mu=0$ and is $\mathbb Z_p$.

= Iwasawa class-number growth theorem
{c}
{parent=Iwasawa invariants}
{title2=$v_p(h_{F_n})=\mu p^n+\lambda n+\nu\quad(n\gg0)$}

For the finite layers of a <Zp-extension> of a <number field>, the p-primary <ideal class groups> eventually satisfy the displayed formula with integer invariants $\mu,\lambda\geq0$ and $\nu\in\mathbb Z$. The first two are the <Iwasawa invariants> of the <unramified Iwasawa module>. The <Iwasawa module structure theorem> and control of the finite-layer norm and ramification terms give the result. For a cyclotomic extension of an abelian field, the <Ferrero-Washington theorem> gives $\mu=0$. This growth theorem records two invariants; it is weaker than identifying the full <characteristic ideal> in the <Iwasawa main conjecture>.

= Ferrero-Washington theorem
{title2=$\mu(Y)=0$}
{c}
{parent=Iwasawa invariants}
{wiki=Ferrero–Washington_theorem}

For a finite abelian extension of $\mathbb Q$, the <unramified Iwasawa module> of its <cyclotomic Zp-extension> has $\mu=0$. This removes $p$-power elementary factors from its characteristic ideal. The theorem does not assert $\mu=0$ for arbitrary noncyclotomic <Zp-extensions>.

= Zp-extension
{c}
{parent=Iwasawa theory}
{title2=$\operatorname{Gal}(F_\infty/F)\cong\mathbb Z_p$}

A Zp-extension is an infinite <Galois extension> of a <number field> whose <Galois group> is topologically isomorphic to the additive <p-adic integers>. There is a unique intermediate field of degree $p^n$ for every $n\geq0$, corresponding to $p^n\mathbb Z_p$. The finite layers form a tower with cyclic successive degree $p$ extensions.

= Universal norm of units in a Zp-extension
{parent=Zp-extension}
{title2=$N_\infty(E_n)=\bigcap_{m\ge n}N_{m/n}(E_m)$}

These are units that lie in every finite-layer norm image, using the p-adic closures of the unit groups. Compactness makes this intersection the image of the norm inverse limit. Local and global universal norm subgroups need not coincide. In the cyclotomic main-conjecture proof, the finite defect of global units from universal norms is related by <class field theory> to invariants of the unramified module.

= Ramification in a Zp-extension
{parent=Zp-extension}

At least one finite prime ramifies in a <Zp-extension>; otherwise the entire tower would lie in a finite <Hilbert class field>. Ramification can occur only over $p$: <local class field theory> makes inertia away from $p$ an image of a unit group with finite maximal pro-$p$ quotient, whereas $\mathbb Z_p$ has no nontrivial finite subgroup. Nonzero inertia is open in $\mathbb Z_p$, so it becomes total after passing to a sufficiently high finite layer.

= Cyclotomic Zp-extension
{parent=Zp-extension}

For odd $p$, remove the finite torsion subgroup $\mu_{p-1}$ from the <Galois group> of $\mathbb Q(\mu_{p^\infty})/\mathbb Q$ by taking its fixed field. For $p=2$, take the fixed field of $\{\pm1\}$ instead. The remaining <Galois group> is $\mathbb Z_p$, using the <p-adic logarithm> on $1+p\mathbb Z_p$ or $1+4\mathbb Z_2$. For a <number field> $K$, the cyclotomic Zp-extension is the compositum with this rational tower.

= Weak approximation for number fields
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

Given finitely many distinct <places of a number field> $v_1,\ldots,v_r$, the diagonal image of $K$ is dense in $\prod_i K_{v_i}$. One can therefore simultaneously prescribe finite congruences and real signs. This is useful in the <ray class exact sequence> and in finding representatives of an <ideal class> prime to a prescribed finite set of <prime ideals>.

= Prescribing inert primes in a quadratic extension
{parent=Weak approximation for number fields}

At finitely many finite places of a <number field>, choose square-class parameters for the local unramified <quadratic extensions>. Local square classes are open, including at dyadic places by the <strong form of Hensel lemma>. <Weak approximation for number fields> gives a global parameter in all the prescribed local classes. Its quadratic extension is unramified of residue degree two at those places, so their extended prime ideals remain prime. If no finite place is prescribed, an auxiliary local nonsquare condition ensures the global extension is nontrivial.

= Algebraic norm
{title2=$N(\alpha)$}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

The algebraic norm of an element of a finite field extension is the determinant of multiplication by that element. In a quadratic subring of $\mathbb C$, it is often $N(\alpha)=\alpha\overline\alpha$.

= Multiplicativity of the algebraic norm
{parent=Algebraic norm}

For algebraic elements $\alpha$ and $\beta$,
$$
N(\alpha\beta)=N(\alpha)N(\beta).
$$
This follows because multiplication by $\alpha\beta$ is the composition of multiplication by $\beta$ and multiplication by $\alpha$, and determinants are multiplicative.

= Hermite–Minkowski theorem
{c}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

For fixed degree and bounded absolute discriminant, there are only finitely many number fields up to isomorphism. In particular, only finitely many extensions of bounded degree can be unramified outside a fixed finite set of primes when their ramification exponents are bounded.

= Bounded-degree extensions with restricted ramification
{parent=Hermite–Minkowski theorem}

Fix a <number field> $K$, a positive integer $d$, and a finite set $S$ of its finite places. There are finitely many <field extensions> $L/K$ of degree at most $d$ unramified outside $S$, inside a fixed <algebraic closure>. To see this, view them as extensions of the <rational numbers> of bounded degree. Their ramified rational primes belong to a fixed finite set. At each of these primes, the <different exponent> is bounded in terms of the local degree and ramification index, giving a bound for the absolute <field discriminant>. The <Hermite–Minkowski theorem> gives finitely many fields up to isomorphism, and each has only finitely many embeddings into the fixed closure. This useful form of arithmetic compactness is stated and proved for $K=\mathbb Q$ in https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.785/2018fa/LectureNotes14.pdf[Andrew Sutherland's number-theory notes, Theorem 14.25]; the preceding reduction gives the general case.

= Number field
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

A number field is a finite <field extension> of the <rational number>[rational numbers].

= Totally imaginary number field
{parent=Number field}
{wiki}

= Totally complex number field
{synonym}

A <number field> is totally imaginary if it has no embedding into the real numbers. Its embeddings into the complex numbers occur entirely in conjugate pairs. This is also called totally complex; it does not assert that every element of the field is nonreal.

= CM-field
{c}
{parent=Totally imaginary number field}
{wiki}

= CM number field
{c}
{synonym}

A CM-field is a <totally imaginary number field> that is a quadratic extension of a <totally real number field>. Its nontrivial automorphism over that real subfield acts as <complex conjugation> in every complex embedding. Imaginary <quadratic number fields> are examples. An arbitrary <totally imaginary number field> need not have such a real subfield of index two.

= Unit index of a CM-field
{parent=CM-field}
{title2=$[\mathcal O_K^\times:\mu_K\mathcal O_{K^+}^\times]\in\{1,2\}$}

For a <CM-field>, put $c$ for conjugation and $E_K=\mathcal O_K^\times$. The map $u\mapsto u/c(u)$ takes values in the cyclic group $\mu_K$ of <roots of unity>: all conjugates have absolute value one, and the <Kronecker theorem on algebraic integers in the unit disk> applies. Its kernel is $E_{K^+}$, while its image on $\mu_K$ is $\mu_K^2$. It therefore embeds $E_K/(E_{K^+}\mu_K)$ into $\mu_K/\mu_K^2$, a group of order two. This proves the index bound without assuming that every unit is a real unit times a root of unity.

= Totally real number field
{parent=Number field}
{wiki}

A number field is totally real when every embedding into the complex numbers has image in the real numbers. The maximal real subfield of a <cyclotomic field> is a basic example. Ordinary <Hilbert class fields> split at real places and therefore retain this property.

= Pure cubic number field
{title2=$K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt[3]{m})$}
{parent=Number field}

A pure cubic number field is a degree-three <number field> generated by a root of $X^3-m$ for a rational number $m$ that is not a rational cube. For a positive <square-free integer> $m>1$, this polynomial is an <Eisenstein polynomial> at any prime dividing $m$. The <ring of integers of a number field> depends on congruences at $3$, as expressed by the <integral basis of a nonexceptional pure cubic field>.

= Quadratic field
{parent=Number field}
{wiki}

= Quadratic number field
{synonym}

A quadratic number field is a degree-two extension of $\mathbb Q$, necessarily of the form $\mathbb Q(\sqrt d)$ for a square-free integer $d\ne0,1$.

= Norm-six equation in the quadratic field of square root ten
{parent=Quadratic field}
{title2=$x^2-10y^2=6$}

The ring of integers is $\mathbb Z[\sqrt{10}]$, its class number is two, and its units are $\pm(3+\sqrt{10})^n$ for $n\in\mathbb Z$. Factoring a norm-six principal ideal above 2 and 3 gives all solutions
$$
x+\sqrt{10}y=\pm\epsilon^n(\epsilon+(-1)^n),\qquad \epsilon=3+\sqrt{10},\quad n\in\mathbb Z.
$$
Restriction to nonnegative $n$ gives only the positive-coordinate branch up to simultaneous sign. For example, $4-\sqrt{10}$ is a norm-six element requiring $n=-1$; adding conjugates repairs a nonnegative-index presentation.

= Imaginary quadratic field
{parent=Quadratic field}
{title2=$K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-d}),\quad d>0$}

An imaginary quadratic field is a <quadratic number field> with no real embedding. Its two complex embeddings are interchanged by complex conjugation. The <endomorphism ring of an elliptic curve> in characteristic zero, when larger than the integers, is an order in such a field; this is <complex multiplication>.

= Fundamental discriminant
{title2=$D$}
{parent=Quadratic field}
{wiki}

A fundamental discriminant is an integer that occurs as the <field discriminant> of a <quadratic number field>. For square-free $d$, it is $d$ when $d\equiv1\pmod4$ and $4d$ otherwise.

= Algebraic integer
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

An algebraic number is an algebraic integer when it satisfies a monic polynomial with integer coefficients. Algebraic integers are closed under addition and multiplication.

= Ring of integers
{title2=$\mathcal O_K$}
{parent=Algebraic integer}
{wiki}

= Ring of integers of a number field
{synonym}

The ring of integers $\mathcal O_K$ of a <number field> $K$ is the ring of all <algebraic integers> in $K$.

= Global unit of a number field
{parent=Ring of integers}
{title2=$E_K=\mathcal O_K^\times$}

= Global unit
{synonym}

A <global unit> and its inverse are algebraic integers in the number <field>. Equivalently its valuation is zero at every nonarchimedean prime. <Global units> embed in <local unit> groups; their p-adic closures can be smaller than the full local groups. This discrepancy is measured by the <class-field unit sequence>.

= Order in a number field
{title2=$[\mathcal O_K:A]<\infty$}
{parent=Ring of integers}

An order in a <number field> $K$ is a subring $A\subseteq\mathcal O_K$ containing $1$ and having full rank $[K:\mathbb Q]$ as an additive <abelian group>. Its finite index in the <ring of integers of a number field> is related to its <discriminant> by the <discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice>.

= Prime ideal factorization
{parent=Ring of integers}

Every nonzero ideal of the <ring of integers of a number field> factors uniquely as a finite product of nonzero <prime ideals>. Thus each $x\in K^\times$ has integer valuations $\operatorname{ord}_{\mathfrak p}(x)$, almost all zero, determined by the factorization of its <principal ideal>.

= Ramification index of a prime ideal
{title2=$e_{\mathfrak p}$}
{parent=Prime ideal factorization}

If $\mathfrak p$ is a prime ideal of $\mathcal O_K$ above a rational prime $p$, its ramification index is the exponent $e_{\mathfrak p}$ of $\mathfrak p$ in the factorization of $(p)$. Equivalently, for the normalized ideal valuation, $e_{\mathfrak p}=v_{\mathfrak p}(p)$.

= Fundamental identity for prime decomposition
{parent=Ramification index of a prime ideal}

Let $B$ be the <integral closure> of a <Dedekind domain> $R$ in a finite separable extension $L/K$ of degree $n$. For a nonzero <prime ideal> $\mathfrak p$ of $R$, write
$$
\mathfrak pB=\prod_{\mathfrak q\mid\mathfrak p}\mathfrak q^{e_{\mathfrak q/\mathfrak p}}
$$
and let $f_{\mathfrak q/\mathfrak p}=[B/\mathfrak q:R/\mathfrak p]$. Then
$$
n=\sum_{\mathfrak q\mid\mathfrak p}e_{\mathfrak q/\mathfrak p}f_{\mathfrak q/\mathfrak p}.
$$

= Ring of integers of Q of pth root p
{title2=$\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt[p]{p})}=\mathbb Z[\sqrt[p]{p}]$}
{parent=Ring of integers}

For a <prime number> $p$ and $\alpha=\sqrt[p]{p}$, the polynomial $X^p-p$ is <Eisenstein> at $p$, and
$$
\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q(\alpha)}=\mathbb Z[\alpha].
$$
The <discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice> reduces the proof to the prime $p$. At the unique prime ideal above $p$, one has $v(\alpha)=1$ and $v(p)=p$, which rules out an integral element $g(\alpha)/p$ with $g\notin p\mathbb Z[X]$.

= Ring of integers of a quadratic field
{parent=Ring of integers}

For square-free $d\ne0,1$, an integral basis is $\{1,(1+\sqrt d)/2\}$ when $d\equiv1\pmod4$, and $\{1,\sqrt d\}$ otherwise. The discriminant is respectively $d$ and $4d$.

= Ring of integers of Q of square root two
{title2=$\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)}=\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]$}
{parent=Ring of integers of a quadratic field}

Since $2\not\equiv1\pmod4$, the ring of integers of $\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)$ is $\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]$, with integral basis $1,\sqrt2$ and field discriminant eight.

= Norm-Euclideanity of the integers adjoined square root two
{parent=Ring of integers of Q of square root two}
{title2=$|N(a+b\sqrt2)|=|a^2-2b^2|$}

The ring $\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]$ is a <Euclidean domain> for the absolute <field norm>. Approximate both rational coefficients of a quotient by nearest integers. If the errors are $u,w\in[-1/2,1/2]$, then $|u^2-2w^2|\leq1/2<1$, giving a remainder of strictly smaller absolute norm. In particular this ring is a <principal ideal domain> and has trivial <ideal class group>.

= Splitting of prime ideals in Galois extensions
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

In a <Galois extension>, the prime ideals above a fixed unramified prime have a common ramification index and residue degree, and the Galois group acts transitively on them.

= Completely split prime
{parent=Splitting of prime ideals in Galois extensions}

A <prime ideal> of a <number field> splits completely in a finite extension of degree $n$ when it factors into $n$ distinct <prime ideals>, each of <residue-field degree> one. In a <Galois extension>, an unramified <prime ideal> splits completely exactly when its <Frobenius conjugacy class> is the identity class. The <Chebotarev density theorem> then gives these primes <Dirichlet density> $1/n$.

= Splitting of rational primes in a quadratic field
{parent=Splitting of prime ideals in Galois extensions}

For a quadratic field of discriminant $D$ and a rational prime $p$, the ideal $(p)$ splits into two distinct prime ideals, remains prime, or ramifies according as the quadratic character $\chi_D(p)$ is $1$, $-1$, or $0$. The corresponding prime-ideal norms are $(p,p)$, $p^2$, and $p$, respectively.

= Ramification of a prime
{parent=Splitting of rational primes in a quadratic field}

<Ramification> specializes to primes as follows: a prime $p$ ramifies in a number field when its prime-ideal factorization contains a factor with exponent greater than one.

= Everywhere unramified extension of number fields
{parent=Ramification of a prime}

A finite extension of <number fields> is unramified everywhere when every finite <prime ideal> is unramified. If infinite places are included in the convention, one also requires every real place to remain real.

= Splitting of three in Q of square root minus p
{parent=Splitting of rational primes in a quadratic field}

If $p\equiv11\pmod{12}$ and $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-p})$, then $\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[(1+\sqrt{-p})/2]$. The generator's minimal polynomial reduces modulo $3$ to $T(T-1)$, so $(3)$ splits into two distinct prime ideals of norm three.

= Discriminant of a biquadratic field
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

The discriminant of a biquadratic field is the product of the fundamental discriminants of its three quadratic subfields.

= Algebraic integer module criterion
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

An element $x$ is integral over $\mathbb Z$ exactly when it acts stably on some faithful nonzero finitely generated $\mathbb Z$-module containing one.

= Ring generated by algebraic integers
{parent=Algebraic integer module criterion}

A ring generated over $\mathbb Z$ by finitely many algebraic integers is a finitely generated $\mathbb Z$-module.

= Integral dependence is transitive
{parent=Algebraic integer module criterion}

If $B$ is integral over $A$ and an element is integral over $B$, then it is integral over $A$.

= Integral basis
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

An integral basis is a $\mathbb Z$-basis of the ring of integers of a number field.

= Integral basis of a cubefree pure cubic field
{parent=Integral basis}
{title2=$1,\alpha,\alpha^2/m_2,\quad\alpha^3=m_1m_2^2$}

Let $m_1,m_2$ be coprime positive <square-free integers>, with $m_1\not\equiv\pm m_2\pmod9$. The displayed <integral basis> has <discriminant> $-27m_1^2m_2^2$. Both nonconstant generators are integral, and their products are integral linear combinations of the three basis elements. At a prime dividing $m_1$, the cubic for $\alpha$ is <Eisenstein>; at a prime dividing $m_2$, the cubic for $\alpha^2/m_2$ is <Eisenstein>. If $3\nmid m_1m_2$, then $m_1m_2^2\not\equiv\pm1\pmod9$, and a shift by $1$ or $-1$ gives an <Eisenstein polynomial> at three. These local <uniformizer> generators show that the order is maximal at all primes dividing its <discriminant>; elsewhere the <discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice> already gives maximality.

= Integral basis of a two-prime biquadratic field
{parent=Integral basis}
{title2=$\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt p),\quad p\equiv3\pmod4$}

For an odd prime $p\equiv3\pmod4$, traces to the three quadratic subfields show that an integer has coefficients $(a+b\sqrt p+c\sqrt2+d\sqrt{2p})/2$ with rational integers $a,b,c,d$. Its norm to $\mathbb Q(\sqrt p)$ forces $a,b$ even and $c\equiv d\pmod2$. Hence an <integral basis> is $1,\sqrt2,\sqrt p,(1+\sqrt p)\sqrt2/2$. The last generator satisfies the monic <polynomial> $X^4-(p+1)X^2+(p-1)^2/4$, proving integrality as well as spanning. The subfield used for the norm matters: the norm to $\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)$ has different coefficients.

= Integral basis of the fourth-root-of-two field
{parent=Integral basis}
{title2=$\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt[4]2)}=\mathbb Z[\sqrt[4]2]$}

Write $\alpha=\sqrt[4]2$ and an algebraic integer as $a+b\alpha+c\alpha^2+d\alpha^3$ with rational coefficients. Traces to $\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)$ show $2a,2b,2c,4d$ are integers. The norm has coefficients $a^2+2c^2-4bd$ and $2ac-b^2-2d^2$ in the integral basis $1,\sqrt2$. Set $A=2a$, $B=2b$, $C=2c$, $D=4d$. Integrality gives $A^2+2C^2-2BD\equiv0\pmod4$ and $4AC-2B^2-D^2\equiv0\pmod8$. The second forces $D$ even and then $B$ even; the first forces $A,C$ even; returning to the second forces $D$ divisible by four. Thus all original coefficients are integers, proving the integral basis $1,\alpha,\alpha^2,\alpha^3$.

= Integral basis of a nonexceptional pure cubic field
{title2=$\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt[3]{m})}=\mathbb Z[\sqrt[3]{m}]$}
{parent=Integral basis}

If $m>1$ is a <square-free integer>, $3\nmid m$, and $m\not\equiv\pm1\pmod9$, then $1,\sqrt[3]{m},\sqrt[3]{m^2}$ is an <integral basis> of the <pure cubic number field>, with <field discriminant> $-27m^2$. The <discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice> restricts a possible index to primes dividing $3m$. At primes dividing $m$ the <different exponent> is $2$ by <tame ramification>. A <shifted Eisenstein polynomial> gives total wild ramification at $3$, so its <different exponent> is at least $3$. These exponents exhaust the polynomial <discriminant> and force index one.

= Squarefree discriminant certifies an integral basis
{parent=Integral basis}

The <discriminant> of an integral full-rank lattice is the field <discriminant> multiplied by the square of its index in the ring of integers. A squarefree lattice <discriminant> therefore forces index one.

= Integral basis of an odd biquadratic field containing square root of two
{parent=Integral basis}
{title2=$1,\sqrt2,\sqrt\delta,(\sqrt2+\sqrt{2\delta})/2$}

For square-free $\delta\equiv3\pmod4$, the field $\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt\delta)$ has basis $1,\sqrt2,\sqrt\delta,(\sqrt2+\sqrt{2\delta})/2$ as an <integral basis> of its ring of <integers>. <Relative traces> force all rational coefficients to be half-integers; the <norm> to $\mathbb Q(\sqrt\delta)$ forces the constant and $\sqrt\delta$ coefficients to be <integers> and the two other numerators to have equal parity. The half-sum is integral because it satisfies $X^4-(1+\delta)X^2+(\delta-1)^2/4=0$.

= Discriminant of elements of a number field
{parent=Integral basis}

For elements $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n$ of a degree-$n$ number field with embeddings $\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_n$, their discriminant is
$$
\operatorname{disc}(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n)
=\det(\sigma_i(\alpha_j))^2
=\det(\operatorname{Tr}_{K/\mathbb Q}(\alpha_i\alpha_j)).
$$
It is nonzero exactly when the elements form a $\mathbb Q$-basis.

= Field discriminant
{title2=$d_K$}
{parent=Integral basis}
{wiki}

The field discriminant is the discriminant of any integral basis and controls the index of every suborder.

= Coprime discriminants imply linear disjointness
{parent=Field discriminant}

Two finite <Galois extensions> of the rational <number field> with coprime <field discriminants> have trivial intersection. Any ramified prime in the intersection would ramify in both fields. The intersection is therefore everywhere unramified at finite primes and has <field discriminant> of absolute value one. The <Minkowski lower bound for a number-field discriminant> excludes this in degree greater than one. The <Galois correspondence> then gives the product degree for their <compositum>. The normality hypothesis is used in this last intersection-to-degree argument.

= Discriminant obstruction to ramification
{parent=Field discriminant}
{title2=$p\text{ ramified}\implies p\mid\Delta_K$}

A ramified prime makes the finite algebra $\mathcal O_K/p\mathcal O_K$ have a nonzero <nilpotent element>. Multiplication by its product with any other element is nilpotent and has trace zero, so the reduced <trace pairing> is degenerate. Therefore the <field discriminant> is divisible by $p$. This proves finiteness of the ramified rational primes without requiring explicit prime factorization in the field.

= Discriminant-index formula for an integral lattice
{parent=Field discriminant}

Let $\omega_1,\ldots,\omega_n$ be an integral basis and let $\alpha_j=\sum_i a_{ij}\omega_i$ be a rational basis of algebraic integers, with integer matrix $A=(a_{ij})$. Their trace Gram matrices satisfy
$$
G_\alpha=A^TG_\omega A,
$$
so
$$
\Delta(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n)
=(\det A)^2d_K.
$$
The absolute discriminant is minimized exactly when $A$ is unimodular, equivalently when the $\alpha_i$ form an integral basis.

= Cubic field of discriminant minus 307
{title2=$K=\mathbb Q(\alpha),\ \alpha^3-5\alpha+8=0$}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

The polynomial $X^3-5X+8$ is irreducible over $\mathbb Q$. For
$$
\beta=\frac{\alpha+\alpha^2}{2},
$$
one has
$$
\beta^3-5\beta^2+11\beta-12=0,
\qquad
\alpha=-4+3\beta-\beta^2.
$$
The field has ring of integers $\mathbb Z[\beta]$ and field discriminant $-307$.

= Integral basis of the cubic field of discriminant minus 307
{parent=Cubic field of discriminant minus 307}

The elements
$$
1,\qquad\alpha,\qquad\beta=\frac{\alpha+\alpha^2}{2}
$$
form an integral basis. Relative to this basis, multiplication by $\beta$ has integer matrix
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
0&-4&-4\\
0&2&-1\\
1&1&3
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The basis discriminant is one quarter of the discriminant $-4\cdot307$ of $(1,\alpha,\alpha^2)$, hence is the square-free integer $-307$.

= Prime ideals above two and three in the cubic field of discriminant minus 307
{parent=Cubic field of discriminant minus 307}

For $g(X)=X^3-5X^2+11X-12$, the reductions are
$$
\overline g=X(X^2+X-1)\quad\text{over }\mathbb F_3
$$
and
$$
\overline g=X(X^2+X+1)\quad\text{over }\mathbb F_2.
$$
Both quadratic factors are irreducible, so
$$
(3)=(3,\beta)(3,\beta^2+\beta-1)
$$
and
$$
(2)=(2,\beta)(2,\beta^2+\beta+1).
$$

= Field norm
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

The field norm $N_{L/K}(x)$ is the determinant of multiplication by $x$, equivalently the product of its conjugates when the extension is separable.

= Norm of an integral element over a normal domain
{parent=Field norm}
{title2=$N_{L/K}(b)\in A$}

Let $A$ be an <integrally closed domain> with fraction field $K$, and let $L/K$ be finite. If $b\in L$ is an <integral element> over $A$, its monic <minimal polynomial> lies in $A[T]$: its roots are integral, so its coefficients are integral elements of $K$, hence belong to $A$. If that polynomial has degree $r$ and constant coefficient $a_0$, then $N_{L/K}(b)=((-1)^ra_0)^{[L:K(b)]}\in A$. This remains true for inseparable extensions. An infinite algebraic extension has no usual determinant field norm.

= Unit norm detects units in a local extension
{parent=Field norm}
{title2=$v_K(N_{L/K}x)=f(L/K)v_L(x)$}

For a finite extension of <local fields> with normalized <discrete valuations>, the <field norm> satisfies the displayed equality. The extended <absolute values on a field> of all conjugates agree, so $|N(x)|_K=|x|_L^{[L:K]}$. Using $[L:K]=e(L/K)f(L/K)$ and $v_L|_K=e(L/K)v_K$ gives the equality. Thus the <field norm> is a unit precisely when the original element is a unit.

= Norm form
{parent=Field norm}
{wiki}

For a finite <number field> $L/\mathbb Q$ with a rational basis $\omega_1,\ldots,\omega_n$, the norm form is the homogeneous polynomial
$$
F(X_1,\ldots,X_n)
=N_{L/\mathbb Q}(X_1\omega_1+\cdots+X_n\omega_n).
$$

= Prime representation by the norm form of Q of square root 229
{parent=Norm form}
{title2=$p=x^2+xy-57y^2$}

For a rational prime $p\ne229$, representation by this <norm form> is equivalent to complete splitting in the <Hilbert class field of Q of square root 229>. Its Frobenius is a permutation of the three roots of $X^3-4X-1$. The quadratic residue condition $(p/229)=1$ makes this permutation even; a root modulo $p$ gives a fixed point, so the even permutation is the identity. Complete splitting makes the prime above $p$ principal. Its generator has norm $\pm p$, and the unit of norm minus one changes the sign to positive, giving the representation.

= Norm obstruction to being a square
{parent=Field norm}

If $x=y^2$ in a number field, then $N(x)=N(y)^2$; a norm that is not a square in the base field therefore obstructs square roots.

= Unit from norm one or minus one
{parent=Field norm}

An algebraic integer whose absolute norm is $1$ or $-1$ is a unit, because its conjugate product supplies an integral inverse.

= Minkowski embedding of a number field
{c}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

For a number field of signature $(r_1,r_2)$, the Minkowski embedding sends an element to its values under the $r_1$ real embeddings and one representative from each of the $r_2$ complex-conjugate pairs. The image of the ring of integers is a full lattice in $\mathbb R^{r_1}\times\mathbb C^{r_2}$.

This construction uses all archimedean embeddings studied in <algebraic number theory>.

= Covolume of a fractional ideal lattice
{title2=$\sqrt{|D_K|}\,N(\mathfrak b)$}
{parent=Minkowski embedding of a number field}

Let $j(\mathfrak b)$ be the <Minkowski embedding of a number field> applied to a nonzero <fractional ideal>. Under the metric $|x|^2=\sum_{v\text{ real}}x_v^2+2\sum_{v\text{ complex}}|x_v|^2$, its <covolume> is $\sqrt{|D_K|}N(\mathfrak b)$. For ordinary coordinate <Lebesgue measure> $\prod dx_v\prod d\operatorname{Re}z_v\,d\operatorname{Im}z_v$, its <covolume> is instead $2^{-r_2}\sqrt{|D_K|}N(\mathfrak b)$. Here $D_K$ is the <field discriminant>, and the absolute <norm of a fractional ideal> is positive and multiplicative. The formula follows by taking the embedding determinant of an <integral basis>, then using the index of an <integral ideal> and scaling to handle a <fractional ideal>.

= Kronecker theorem on algebraic integers in the unit disk
{c}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

A nonzero algebraic integer whose conjugates all have modulus at most one is a <root of unity>. Its norm forces every conjugate to have modulus one, and bounded integral coefficient vectors show that two positive powers coincide.

= Minkowski's theorem
{c}
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

= Minkowski convex body theorem
{c}
{synonym}

If $\Lambda\subset\mathbb R^n$ is a full lattice and a measurable convex centrally symmetric set $C$ has volume greater than $2^n\operatorname{covol}(\Lambda)$, then $C$ contains a nonzero lattice point.

= Minkowski embedding of a real quadratic field
{parent=Minkowski's theorem}

For a real quadratic field $K$ with embeddings $\sigma_1,\sigma_2$, the image
$$
\{(\sigma_1(\alpha),\sigma_2(\alpha)):\alpha\in\mathcal O_K\}
$$
is a lattice in $\mathbb R^2$ of covolume $\sqrt{|d_K|}$, and the product of the two coordinates is the field norm.

= Imaginary quadratic Minkowski disk bound
{parent=Minkowski's theorem}

Let $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-d})$ for square-free $d>0$ with $d\not\equiv3\pmod4$, so $\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[\sqrt{-d}]$. An integral ideal $I$ is a lattice in $\mathbb C\simeq\mathbb R^2$ of covolume $\sqrt d\,N(I)$. Applying the <Minkowski convex body theorem> to a disk gives a nonzero $\alpha\in I$ satisfying
$$
0<|N_{K/\mathbb Q}(\alpha)|
\le\frac{4\sqrt d}{\pi}N(I).
$$

= Mahler measure
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

If $f(X)=a_d\prod_{j=1}^d(X-\alpha_j)$, its Mahler measure is
$$
M(f)=|a_d|\prod_{j=1}^d\max\{1,|\alpha_j|\}.
$$

= Mahler-measure norm argument at measure two
{parent=Mahler measure}

Let an irreducible $f\in\mathbb Z[X]$ have $M(f)=2$ and a root outside the closed unit disk. Its leading coefficient must have absolute value one. The product $B$ of all roots outside the unit disk is then an algebraic integer with $|B|=2$; conjugate pairs make $B$ real, so $B=\pm2$. The quotient of the norm of any root by $B$ is both rational and an algebraic integer, and its absolute value is at most one. It is therefore $\pm1$, so every root has absolute norm two.

= Mahler measure bounded by polynomial length
{parent=Mahler measure}

For every complex polynomial $P$,
$$
M(P)\leq\mathcal L(P).
$$
This follows from Jensen's formula, or directly by bounding $|P(z)|$ by its <polynomial length> on the unit circle and using the integral formula for the <Mahler measure>.

= Place of a number field
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

= Places of a number field
{synonym}

A place of a <number field> $K$ is an equivalence class of <absolute values on a field>. Its Archimedean places come from real embeddings and conjugate pairs of complex embeddings. Its non-Archimedean places correspond to nonzero prime ideals of the <ring of integers of a number field>.

Equivalence is taken among <absolute values on a field>, so changing a representative within one place does not change the associated topology.

= Embedding of a number field into a p-adic algebraic closure
{parent=Place of a number field}
{title2=$\iota:K\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathbb Q}_p$}

= Embeddings of a number field into a p-adic algebraic closure
{synonym}

Such a <field embedding> pulls the <p-adic absolute value> back to a finite <place of a number field> above $p$. It extends to an embedding of the corresponding completion, whose image is a finite extension of the <p-adic numbers>.

= Embedding orbit description of finite places
{parent=Embedding of a number field into a p-adic algebraic closure}

Finite <places of a number field> above $p$ correspond to absolute <Galois group> orbits of <embeddings of a number field into a p-adic algebraic closure>. The absolute value is invariant under the group. Conversely, embeddings inducing the same place extend to embeddings of the same local completion and are conjugate.

= Archimedean place
{parent=Place of a number field}
{wiki}

A real embedding $K\hookrightarrow\mathbb R$ gives a real Archimedean place of local degree $1$. A conjugate pair of complex embeddings $K\hookrightarrow\mathbb C$ gives a complex Archimedean place of local degree $2$.

= Totally positive element of a number field
{title2=$\sigma_v(a)>0\text{ at every real place}$}
{parent=Archimedean place}

= Totally positive
{synonym}

A nonzero element of a <number field> is totally positive when its image is positive under every real <field embedding>. In a totally real <number field>, the <principal fractional ideals> having a <totally positive> generator form the subgroup used to define the <narrow ideal class group>. Signs of different generators are related by the <unit signature map>.

= Non-Archimedean place of a number field
{parent=Place of a number field}

If a prime ideal $\mathfrak p$ of $K$ lies over the <prime number> $p$ with <ramification index of a prime ideal> $e_{\mathfrak p}$, its normalized absolute value is
$$
|x|_{\mathfrak p}=p^{-\operatorname{ord}_{\mathfrak p}(x)/e_{\mathfrak p}}.
$$
It extends the usual <p-adic absolute value> on $\mathbb Q$. Its local degree is $[K_{\mathfrak p}:\mathbb Q_p]=e_{\mathfrak p}f_{\mathfrak p}$, where $f_{\mathfrak p}$ is the <residue-field degree>.

= Local degree of a place
{parent=Place of a number field}

For a place $v$ of a <number field> $K$, the local degree is $d_v=[K_v:\mathbb Q_v]$. Thus $d_v=1$ at a real place, $d_v=2$ at a complex place, and $d_v=e_vf_v$ at a finite place over $p$. For each rational place, the local degrees above it sum to $[K:\mathbb Q]$.

= Height function
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

= Arithmetic height
{synonym}

A height function measures the arithmetic complexity of an algebraic number or a point of an arithmetic variety.

= Néron–Tate height
{c}
{parent=Height function}
{wiki}
{title2=$\widehat h_L(P)$}

For a symmetric ample line bundle $L$ on an <abelian variety>, the canonical height is $\widehat h_L(P)=\lim_{n\to\infty}4^{-n}h_L([2^n]P)$. It is quadratic and differs from the associated logarithmic Weil height by a bounded function. The <canonical height of an elliptic curve> specializes this construction to a one-dimensional abelian variety.

= Weil height machine
{parent=Height function}
{c}
{title2=$L\longmapsto h_L\pmod{O(1)}$}

On a projective variety over a <number field>, a very ample <line bundle> gives a logarithmic <projective height> by an embedding. Tensor products correspond to sums by the Segre embedding, and a sufficiently ample twist expresses any line bundle as a difference of very ample ones. This defines its height modulo bounded functions. It is additive and functorial under morphism pullback. An ample bundle has the <Northcott theorem> finiteness property for bounded height and bounded point degree; a trivial bundle need not. The <product formula> makes these absolute heights independent of scalar coordinates and of the number field used to compute them.

= Absolute multiplicative Weil height
{c}
{title2=$H(\alpha)$}
{parent=Height function}

= Multiplicative height
{synonym}

For an algebraic number $\alpha$ in a number field $L$, the absolute multiplicative Weil height is
$$
H(\alpha)=\prod_{v\in M_L}\max(1,|\alpha|_v)^{[L_v:\mathbb Q_v]/[L:\mathbb Q]}.
$$
It is independent of the field $L$ containing $\alpha$, satisfies $H(\alpha^n)=H(\alpha)^{|n|}$, and obeys $H(\alpha+\beta)\leq2H(\alpha)H(\beta)$.

= Height of a rational number
{parent=Absolute multiplicative Weil height}

For coprime integers $p,q$ with $q>0$,
$$
H(p/q)=\max\{|p|,q\}.
$$
This follows directly from the real and <p-adic absolute value>[p-adic absolute values], or from the <height-Mahler measure formula> for the primitive polynomial $qX-p$.

= Absolute logarithmic Weil height
{c}
{title2=$h(\alpha)$}
{parent=Absolute multiplicative Weil height}

= Logarithmic height
{synonym}

The absolute logarithmic Weil height is $h(\alpha)=\log H(\alpha)$.

= Projective height
{title2=$H_p$}
{parent=Absolute multiplicative Weil height}

For a nonzero vector $\mathbf a=(a_0,\ldots,a_n)$ over a <number field> $K$, its absolute projective height is
$$
H_p(\mathbf a)=\prod_{v\in M_K}\max_i|a_i|_v^{d_v/[K:\mathbb Q]}.
$$
The <product formula> makes this unchanged by multiplying all coordinates by the same nonzero scalar. The projective height of a <linear form> is the projective height of its coefficient vector.

= Naive polynomial height
{title2=$H(P)$}
{parent=Projective height}

The naive height of a polynomial is the largest absolute value of its coefficients. For a primitive polynomial in $\mathbb Z[X_1,\ldots,X_n]$, this equals the <projective height> of its coefficient vector.

= Height-Mahler measure formula
{parent=Absolute multiplicative Weil height}

If the primitive minimal polynomial of an algebraic number $\alpha$ has degree $d$, then
$$
H(\alpha)^d=M(f_\alpha).
$$

= Height bound for a polynomial evaluation
{parent=Absolute multiplicative Weil height}

Let $P,Q\in\mathbb Z[X_1,\ldots,X_k]$ have degree at most $n_j$ in $X_j$. Then
$$
H\left(\frac{P(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_k)}
{Q(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_k)}\right)
\leq
\max\{\mathcal L(P),\mathcal L(Q)\}
\prod_{j=1}^kH(\alpha_j)^{n_j}
$$
whenever the quotient is defined. At each non-Archimedean place, the integral coefficients contribute at most one; at the Archimedean places, the <triangle inequality> contributes the <polynomial length>. Multiplying these local estimates and using the <product formula> gives the claim.

= Liouville height inequality
{c}
{parent=Absolute multiplicative Weil height}

If $\gamma\ne0$ is algebraic of degree at most $D$, then every embedding satisfies
$$
|\gamma|\geq H(\gamma)^{-D}.
$$
Indeed, $H(\gamma^{-1})=H(\gamma)$ and the contribution of one Archimedean place to the height bounds $|\gamma|^{-1/D}$.

= Northcott theorem
{c}
{parent=Absolute multiplicative Weil height}

= Northcott lemma
{c}
{synonym}

There are only finitely many algebraic numbers of bounded <field extension>[degree] and bounded <absolute multiplicative Weil height>. The finite set can be enumerated effectively from the bounded coefficients of their primitive minimal polynomials.

The degree and <absolute multiplicative Weil height> bounds are both essential.

= Minimal-polynomial coefficient proof of Northcott finiteness
{parent=Northcott theorem}
{title2=$|a_j|\leq2^D B^D\quad\text{if }[\mathbb Q(\alpha):\mathbb Q]\leq D,\ H(\alpha)\leq B$}

For the primitive minimal polynomial $a_d\prod_j(T-\alpha_j)$, the <height-Mahler measure formula> gives $H(\alpha)^d=a_d\prod_j\max(1,|\alpha_j|)$. Expanding the product bounds every integer coefficient by $2^D B^D$. There are finitely many bounded coefficient lists and each has finitely many roots, proving finiteness of the algebraic numbers. On a projective coordinate chart, every coordinate ratio has height at most the point's <projective height>; applying the scalar bound proves the projective <Northcott theorem> too. A degree bound is essential, as the infinitely many <roots of unity> all have height one.

= Product formula
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

For a nonzero element $x$ of a <number field> $L$, normalized absolute values satisfy
$$
\prod_{v\in M_L}|x|_v^{[L_v:\mathbb Q_v]}=1.
$$
For an algebraic integer $x$, factor the principal ideal $(x)$ into prime ideals. Its ideal norm is both the product of the finite-place contributions with inverse exponent and the absolute value of the product of its Archimedean conjugates. Equating the two expressions proves the formula for algebraic integers, and writing an arbitrary $x\in L^\times$ as a quotient proves the general case.

= Normalized local factors for the product formula
{parent=Product formula}
{title2=$|x|_{\mathfrak p}=(N\mathfrak p)^{-\operatorname{ord}_{\mathfrak p}(x)}$}

The <product formula> uses finite-place factors given by <ideal norms>, the ordinary modulus at real embeddings, and the modulus squared at complex places. The complex square accounts for the pair of conjugate embeddings. Equivalently ordinary complex modulus can be used with weight two in the product. These normalizations cancel the absolute global <field norm>.

= Dirichlet's unit theorem
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

= Dirichlet unit theorem
{c}
{synonym}

If a number field has $r$ real embeddings and $s$ conjugate pairs of complex embeddings, then
$$
\mathcal O_K^\times\cong\mu(K)\times\mathbb Z^{r+s-1},
$$
where $\mu(K)$ is the finite group of roots of unity in $K$.

= Small archimedean value of a large unit
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}
{title2=$\min_\sigma\log|\sigma(u)|\le-c\|b\|_\infty$}

Write $u=\zeta\prod\varepsilon_i^{b_i}$ in a fixed fundamental-unit basis. The logarithmic embedding is injective on the exponent lattice and lands in the weighted sum-zero hyperplane. Norm equivalence gives a logarithmic coordinate of size at least a constant times $\|b\|_\infty$; the weighted sum-zero relation then forces a negative coordinate of comparable size. There are only finitely many torsion choices. This exponential smallness, together with $\alpha u+\beta v=1$ and a <linear form in logarithms of algebraic numbers> for the nearby value $\beta v$, bounds the <unit> exponents effectively.

= Fundamental unit
{disambiguate=number theory}
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{wiki}

= Fundamental unit
{synonym}

In a real quadratic <number field>, a fundamental unit $\epsilon>1$ generates the infinite cyclic factor of its <unit group>, so every <unit> is $\pm\epsilon^n$. Existence follows from the <Dirichlet unit theorem>; choosing the positive generator of the rank-one logarithmic lattice gives this normalization.

= Obstruction to norm-minus-one units from a prime congruent to three modulo four
{parent=Fundamental unit (number theory)}

If a squarefree positive $d$ has a prime divisor $p\equiv3\pmod4$, a <unit> of norm $-1$ in $\mathbb Q(\sqrt d)$ would give $a^2-db^2=-4$ for integers $a,b$. Modulo $p$ this makes $-1$ a <quadratic residue>, which is impossible. The obstruction is sufficient, not a full criterion for negative-norm units.

= Units of the quadratic field Q square root of five
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}

For $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt5)$,
$$
\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z\left[\frac{1+\sqrt5}{2}\right].
$$
Writing $\phi=(1+\sqrt5)/2$, the full unit group is
$$
\mathcal O_K^\times=\{\mathord\pm\phi^n:n\in\mathbb Z\}.
$$

= Units of Q of square root two
{title2=$\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)}^\times$}
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}

The unit $\varepsilon=1+\sqrt2$ has norm minus one and is the least unit greater than one in the positive real embedding. <Dirichlet unit theorem> therefore gives
$$
\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]^\times
=\{\mathord\pm(1+\sqrt2)^n:n\in\mathbb Z\}.
$$

= Units congruent to one modulo 3 plus square root two
{title2=$\{u\in\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]^\times:u\equiv1\pmod{3+\sqrt2}\}$}
{parent=Units of Q of square root two}

Reduction modulo $(3+\sqrt2)$ identifies $\mathbb Z[\sqrt2]/(3+\sqrt2)$ with $\mathbb F_7$, sends $\sqrt2$ to $4$, and sends $\varepsilon=1+\sqrt2$ to $5$. Since $5$ has order six and $5^3=-1$ in $\mathbb F_7^\times$, the kernel on units is the infinite cyclic group
$$
\left\langle-(1+\sqrt2)^3\right\rangle
=\langle-7-5\sqrt2\rangle.
$$

= Finite relative unit quotient over a real quadratic field
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}

If $K$ is real quadratic and $L/K$ is a number-field extension with $\mathcal O_L^\times/\mathcal O_K^\times$ finite, then the unit ranks agree. If $d=[L:K]$ and $L$ has signature $(r_1,r_2)$, this forces
$$
r_1+r_2=2,\qquad r_1+2r_2=2d.
$$
For a proper extension the only possibility is $d=2$ and signature $(0,2)$. For $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt5)$, the example $L=K(i)$ has this signature and a nontrivial finite quotient because $i$ is a new root of unity.

= Logarithmic embedding of number field units
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}

Choose the real embeddings $\sigma_i$ and one embedding $\tau_j$ from each complex-conjugate pair. The weighted logarithmic embedding is
$$
\operatorname{Log}(u)=
\bigl(\log|\sigma_1u|,\ldots,\log|\sigma_ru|,
2\log|\tau_1u|,\ldots,2\log|\tau_su|\bigr).
$$
For a unit, the sum of these coordinates is $\log|N_{K/\mathbb Q}(u)|=0$.

= Kernel of the logarithmic unit embedding
{parent=Logarithmic embedding of number field units}

The kernel of $\operatorname{Log}:\mathcal O_K^\times\to\mathbb R^{r+s}$ is exactly $\mu(K)$. If every conjugate of a unit has modulus one, all its powers lie in a bounded subset of the discrete Minkowski lattice $\mathcal O_K$, so two powers coincide and the unit is a root of unity.

= Dirichlet unit lattice
{parent=Logarithmic embedding of number field units}
{c}

The logarithms of a fundamental system of $r+s-1$ units form a basis of the hyperplane
$$
H=\{x\in\mathbb R^{r+s}:x_1+\cdots+x_{r+s}=0\},
$$
and their integer span is a full lattice in $H$.

= Archimedean balancing of a principal ideal generator
{parent=Dirichlet unit lattice}

Every principal ideal $I$ of a number field has a generator $\alpha$ satisfying
$$
|\sigma(\alpha)|\leq C_KN(I)^{1/[K:\mathbb Q]}
$$
at every archimedean embedding. Multiply an arbitrary generator by a unit so that its logarithmic unit-lattice coordinates lie in a fixed bounded fundamental parallelepiped.

= Units of Q of square root ten
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}

For $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{10})$,
$$
\mathcal O_K^\times
=\{\mathord\pm(3+\sqrt{10})^n:n\in\mathbb Z\}.
$$
The fundamental unit $3+\sqrt{10}$ has norm minus one.

= Units of Q of square root minus three
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}

For $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-3})$ and $\omega=(-1+\sqrt{-3})/2$,
$$
\mathcal O_K^\times
=\{\mathord\pm1,\mathord\pm\omega,\mathord\pm\omega^2\}
\cong C_6.
$$

= Unit group of the fifth cyclotomic field
{parent=Dirichlet's unit theorem}

For a primitive fifth root of unity $\zeta$,
$$
\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q(\zeta)}^\times
=\left\{\mathord\pm\zeta^a
\left(\frac{1+\sqrt5}{2}\right)^b:a,b\in\mathbb Z\right\}
\cong C_{10}\times\mathbb Z.
$$

= Units of the fifth cyclotomic field are real up to roots of unity
{parent=Unit group of the fifth cyclotomic field}

Let $L=\mathbb Q(\zeta_5)$ and let complex conjugation be denoted by a bar. For $u\in\mathcal O_L^\times$, every conjugate of $u/\bar u$ has modulus one, so the <kernel of the logarithmic unit embedding> shows that $u/\bar u$ is a root of unity. Reduction modulo $1-\zeta_5$ gives $u/\bar u\equiv1$, so this root belongs to $\mu_5$. Squaring permutes $\mu_5$, hence multiplication by a suitable root of unity makes $u$ real. The real unit belongs to $\mathbb Q(\sqrt5)$ and the <units of the quadratic field Q square root of five> finish the calculation.

= Class field theory
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

= Global class field theory
{synonym}

Global class field theory classifies finite abelian extensions of a number field using ideal-class or idele-class quotients and the Artin reciprocity map.

= Hasse norm theorem
{c}
{parent=Class field theory}
{wiki}
{title2=$K^\times\cap N_{L/K}J_L=N_{L/K}L^\times\quad(L/K\text{ cyclic})$}

For a cyclic extension of <number fields>, an element of the base field is a global <field norm> exactly when it is a norm at every finite and infinite completion. The <splitting criterion for a cyclic algebra> encodes the norm obstruction as a <Brauer group> class. The <Albert-Brauer-Hasse-Noether theorem> makes a class that splits at every completion split globally, giving the result. Cyclicity is essential; arbitrary noncyclic extensions can have additional local-global norm obstructions.

= Artin L-function
{parent=Class field theory}
{c}

An Artin L-function encodes traces of Frobenius elements in a Galois representation and converts character sums over primes into analytic information.

= Local factor of an Artin L-function
{parent=Artin L-function}
{title2=$L_{\mathfrak p}(\rho,s)=\det(1-(N\mathfrak p)^{-s}\rho(\operatorname{Frob}_{\mathfrak p})|_{V^{I_{\mathfrak p}}})^{-1}$}

Only the <inertia group> invariants enter an <Artin L-function> local factor. A <Frobenius automorphism> lift acts unambiguously on this subspace, since other lifts differ by inertia. For an unramified one-dimensional <group representation>, the factor is $(1-\chi(\operatorname{Frob}_{\mathfrak p})(N\mathfrak p)^{-s})^{-1}$; for a ramified one-dimensional <group representation>, the invariant subspace is zero and the factor is one. This explains the zero values used at conductor primes by quadratic <Dirichlet characters>.

= Local Artin factors of the standard representation of S3
{c}
{parent=Local factor of an Artin L-function}
{title2=$1/(1-t)^2,\quad1/(1-t^2),\quad1/(1+t+t^2)$}

For the <standard representation of the symmetric group> $S_3$, the unramified <local factor of an Artin L-function>, with $t=p^{-s}$, is respectively the displayed factor for identity, transposition, and three-cycle <Frobenius cycle types>. These follow by removing the trivial summand from the three-point <permutation representation>. If the <inertia group> contains a three-cycle, its invariant subspace in the standard representation is zero, so the ramified local factor is one.

= Artin L-function order at one
{c}
{parent=Artin L-function}
{title2=$\operatorname{ord}_{s=1}L_K(\chi,s)=-\langle\chi,1_G\rangle_G$}

Assume that every nontrivial one-dimensional <Artin L-function> over the intermediate fields is holomorphic and nonzero at one, while the trivial one is the base <Dedekind zeta function> with a simple pole. By <Brauer induction>, $\chi=\sum_jn_j\operatorname{Ind}_{H_j}^G\psi_j$ with integers $n_j$ and <linear characters> $\psi_j$. Compatibility of <Artin L-functions> with <induced representations> expresses $L_K(\chi,s)$ as the corresponding product of $L_{L^{H_j}}(\psi_j,s)^{n_j}$. Its order at one is $-\sum_jn_j\langle\psi_j,1_{H_j}\rangle$. <Frobenius reciprocity> turns this sum into $-\langle\chi,1_G\rangle$. Hence every nontrivial <irreducible character> gives order zero and a holomorphic nonzero function near one. This local conclusion does not assert global Artin holomorphy.

= Main theorem of class field theory
{parent=Class field theory}
{c}

The ray class field realizes the ray class group through the Artin reciprocity map and is the maximal abelian extension with bounded conductor.

= Modulus of a number field
{parent=Class field theory}

A modulus is a formal product $\mathfrak m=\mathfrak m_0\mathfrak m_\infty$, where $\mathfrak m_0$ is a nonzero integral ideal and $\mathfrak m_\infty$ is a product of distinct real places.

= Multiplicity of a place in a modulus
{title2=$m_v(\mathfrak c)$}
{parent=Modulus of a number field}

For a <modulus of a number field> $\mathfrak c=\mathfrak c_0\mathfrak c_\infty$, the multiplicity at a finite place is the exponent of its <prime ideal> in $\mathfrak c_0$. At a real <Archimedean place> it is one if that place occurs in $\mathfrak c_\infty$ and zero otherwise. Complex places have multiplicity zero. These effective modulus multiplicities encode congruences and positivity requirements in the <ray class group>.

= Ray class group
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}_{\mathfrak m}(K)$}
{parent=Modulus of a number field}
{wiki}

= Generalised ideal class group
{synonym}

= Generalized ideal class group
{synonym}

The ray class group modulo $\mathfrak m$ is $I_K(\mathfrak m)/P_K(\mathfrak m)$, where the numerator consists of fractional ideals prime to $\mathfrak m_0$ and the denominator consists of $(\alpha)$ with $\alpha\equiv1$ at the finite modulus and positive at every real place in $\mathfrak m_\infty$.

= Congruence subgroup of fractional ideals
{parent=Ray class group}
{title2=$P_K(\mathfrak m)\subseteq H\subseteq I_K(\mathfrak m)$}

A subgroup of the group of <fractional ideals> prime to a <modulus of a number field>, containing the principal ray subgroup. It is the inverse image of a subgroup of the <ray class group>. The <existence theorem of global class field theory> realizes it as the kernel of an <Artin map>, and identifies its quotient with the corresponding <Galois group>. This is the ideal-theoretic class-field meaning of congruence subgroup.

= Ray unit
{title2=$U_{\mathfrak c}=\mathcal O_K^\times\cap K_{\mathfrak c,1}^\times$}
{parent=Ray class group}

A ray unit for a <modulus of a number field> is a unit congruent to one modulo every finite prime power in the modulus and positive at every real place in it. The group of <ray units> is the <kernel> of the <unit group> map to the <residue and signature group of a modulus>.

= Ray class exact sequence
{parent=Ray class group}

For a finite modulus $\mathfrak m$, there is an exact sequence
$$
\mathcal O_K^\times\longrightarrow
(\mathcal O_K/\mathfrak m)^\times\longrightarrow
\operatorname{Cl}_{\mathfrak m}(K)\longrightarrow
\operatorname{Cl}(K)\longrightarrow1.
$$
With real places in the modulus, the residue-unit term and the unit map also record the required signs.

= Unit-surjectivity criterion for a trivial finite ray class group
{parent=Ray class exact sequence}

For a modulus with no infinite component, suppose the ordinary <ideal class group> is trivial. Then the <ray class group> is trivial exactly when the global <unit group> maps surjectively onto the product of local unit residue groups at the finite modulus. Negative real units are allowed: positivity restrictions occur only when the modulus includes the corresponding real places.

= Residue and signature group of a modulus
{title2=$G_{\mathfrak c}=(\mathcal O_K/\mathfrak c_0)^\times\times\{\pm1\}^{\mathfrak c_\infty}$}
{parent=Ray class exact sequence}

This group records finite unit residues and signs at the selected real places of a <modulus of a number field>. Omit the residue factor for trivial finite modulus. Its quotient by the image of the <unit group> is naturally the <kernel> of the map from the <ray class group> to the ordinary <ideal class group>. The <weak approximation for number fields> theorem supplies elements realizing the specified data, and units account for changing a generator of a <principal fractional ideal>.

= Ray class number formula
{parent=Ray class group}

If $U_{\mathfrak m,1}$ consists of units congruent to one modulo $\mathfrak m_0$ and positive at the selected real places, then
$$
|\operatorname{Cl}_{\mathfrak m}(K)|
=h_K\frac{2^{|\mathfrak m_\infty|}N\mathfrak m_0\prod_{\mathfrak p\mid\mathfrak m_0}(1-N\mathfrak p^{-1})}
{[\mathcal O_K^\times:U_{\mathfrak m,1}]}.
$$

= Artin symbol
{title2=$\left(\frac{L/K}{\mathfrak p}\right)$}
{parent=Class field theory}
{c}
{wiki}

For an unramified prime $\mathfrak p$ in an abelian extension $L/K$, the Artin symbol is the unique Galois element acting on residue fields by $x\mapsto x^{N\mathfrak p}$.

= Ideal-theoretic decomposition law
{parent=Artin symbol}

For an unramified prime $\mathfrak p$ in a finite abelian extension $L/K$, the residue degree equals the order of its <Artin symbol>. Consequently the number of primes above $\mathfrak p$ is $[L:K]$ divided by that order.

= Artin reciprocity law
{parent=Artin symbol}
{c}
{wiki}

= Artin reciprocity
{c}
{synonym}

= Artin reciprocity map
{synonym}
{c}

= Artin map
{c}
{synonym}

The Artin map sends an ideal prime to the ramified primes to the product of its Frobenius elements. For a suitable modulus, its kernel is the norm or congruence subgroup associated with the abelian extension.

= Idelic local-global reciprocity equivalence
{parent=Artin reciprocity law}
{title2=$\theta_{L/K}((a_v))=\prod_v\operatorname{rec}_v(a_v)$}

For a finite <abelian extension> of <number fields>, local reciprocity maps into the local <decomposition groups> combine to a continuous map on the <idele group>: outside finitely many places they kill units, and an idele has nonunit components only finitely often. The <Artin reciprocity law> is equivalent to the compatibility that this product kills every <principal idele>. Conversely, restrict a global continuous Artin homomorphism to each single-place embedding of $K_v^*$. These maps reconstruct the global map, by density of finite-component ideles and triviality on a basic open subgroup of units. Merely having unrelated local maps omits the essential principal-idele compatibility.

= Restriction and norm compatibility of the Artin map
{parent=Artin reciprocity law}
{title2=$\operatorname{Art}_{LE/E}(\mathfrak a)|_L=\operatorname{Art}_{L/K}(N_{E/K}\mathfrak a)$}

For an <abelian extension> $L/K$ of <number fields>, restriction to an intermediate <field> takes each <Artin symbol> to that of the smaller extension. For a finite base extension $E/K$, restriction identifies $\operatorname{Gal}(LE/E)$ with $\operatorname{Gal}(L/(L\cap E))$. At an unramified prime $\mathfrak q$ of $E$ above $\mathfrak p$, its residue-field Frobenius restricts to the $f(\mathfrak q/\mathfrak p)$th power of the <Frobenius automorphism> at $\mathfrak p$. Since $N_{E/K}\mathfrak q=\mathfrak p^{f(\mathfrak q/\mathfrak p)}$, the <group homomorphism> property proves the displayed identity for <fractional ideals> supported over unramified primes.

= Conductor of an abelian extension
{parent=Class field theory}

The conductor $\mathfrak f_{L/K}$ is the smallest modulus through which the <Artin reciprocity map> for a finite abelian extension $L/K$ factors. Its finite prime divisors are exactly the ramified primes, with exponents measuring the depth of ramification.

= Conductor-discriminant formula
{parent=Conductor of an abelian extension}
{title2=$\mathfrak d_{L/K}=\prod_{\chi}\mathfrak f(\chi)$}

For a finite <abelian extension> of <number fields>, its <relative discriminant> <ideal> is the product of the finite conductor <ideals> of the characters of its <Galois group>, with the <trivial character> contributing one. In a multiquadratic extension of $\mathbb Q$, the nontrivial characters correspond to its quadratic subfields, and their conductors are the <absolute values> of the corresponding <fundamental discriminants>. This gives an efficient discriminant computation and can prove that a compositum is unramified over a quadratic subfield.

= Existence theorem of global class field theory
{parent=Class field theory}

Finite abelian extensions of $K$ correspond contravariantly to congruence subgroups $H$ with $P_K(\mathfrak m)\subseteq H\subseteq I_K(\mathfrak m)$, and the Artin map identifies $I_K(\mathfrak m)/H$ with the Galois group.

= Ray class field
{parent=Existence theorem of global class field theory}
{wiki}

The ray class field modulo $\mathfrak m$ is the maximal abelian extension whose Artin map factors through the ray class group; its degree is the ray class number.

= Ray class field of Q of square root minus six modulo square root minus six
{parent=Ray class field}
{title2=$K_{(\sqrt{-6})}=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt{-3})$}

For $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-6})$, reduction of $\mathbb Z[\sqrt{-6}]$ modulo $(\sqrt{-6})$ gives $\mathbb Z/6\mathbb Z$. Its <unit group> is covered by the units $\{1,-1\}$ of $K$. The <ray class exact sequence> therefore identifies the <ray class group> with the ordinary <ideal class group>, of order two. Consequently this <ray class field> is the <Hilbert class field of Q of square root minus six>. To verify its lack of <ramification>, the integral basis candidate $1,\sqrt2,b,\sqrt2b$, where $b=(1+\sqrt{-3})/2$, has <discriminant> $8^2(-3)^2=576$. The relative discriminant formula also forces the field discriminant to be divisible by $(-24)^2=576$. Equality proves that the relative <discriminant ideal> is trivial.

= Ray class field of Q of square root minus three modulo seven
{parent=Ray class field}
{title2=$K_{(7)}=\mathbb Q(\zeta_{21}),\quad K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-3})$}

The <ring of integers> is $\mathbb Z[\omega]$, where $\omega^2+\omega+1=0$, and its <class number> is one by the <Minkowski bound for ideal classes>. Modulo seven it is $\mathbb F_7\times\mathbb F_7$. The six <roots of unity> reduce to the kernel of the product map on the two residue <unit groups>, so the <ray class group> is $\mathbb F_7^\times$, of order six. The relative <Artin symbol> on $K(\zeta_7)$ is exponentiation by the <norm of a fractional ideal> modulo seven. It kills the principal ray subgroup, and $[K(\zeta_7):K]=6$, proving that this is the <ray class field>.

= Ray class fields of the rational numbers
{parent=Ray class field}
{title2=$\mathbb Q_{(m)}=\mathbb Q(\zeta_m+\zeta_m^{-1}),\quad\mathbb Q_{(m)\infty}=\mathbb Q(\zeta_m)$}

= Rational ray class fields
{synonym}

For a positive integer $m$, the finite <ray class group> of $\mathbb Q$ is $(\mathbb Z/m\mathbb Z)^\times/\langle-1\rangle$: the <unit group> permits either sign of a rational generator. Its <ray class field> is the maximal real subfield of the <cyclotomic field> $\mathbb Q(\zeta_m)$. Including the real place requires positive principal generators and gives the full group $(\mathbb Z/m\mathbb Z)^\times$ and full <cyclotomic field>. Thus modulus $(5)$ gives $\mathbb Q(\sqrt5)$, while $(5)\infty$ gives $\mathbb Q(\zeta_5)$. The <Artin reciprocity map> sends a prime $\ell\nmid m$ to the exponent $\ell$, taken modulo $\{\pm1\}$ in the finite case. Ramification at real places is possible only when those places are included in the <modulus of a number field>.

= Adele ring
{title2=$\mathbb A_K$}
{parent=Class field theory}
{wiki}

The adele ring is the restricted product of the completions $K_v$ with respect to the valuation rings $\mathcal O_v$ at finite places.

= Restricted product
{title2=$\prod_i'(X_i,U_i)$}
{parent=Adele ring}
{wiki}

Given spaces $X_i$ with distinguished open subsets $U_i$, their restricted product consists of tuples $(x_i)$ for which $x_i\in U_i$ at all but finitely many indices.

= Restricted product topology
{parent=Restricted product}

A basic open set in a restricted product $\prod_v'(X_v,U_v)$ is a product of open local sets that equals the distinguished subset $U_v$ at all but finitely many indices.

= Idele group
{title2=$\mathbb I_K$}
{parent=Adele ring}

The idele group is the restricted product $\prod_v'K_v^\times$ with respect to $\mathcal O_v^\times$ at finite places.

= Principal idele
{parent=Idele group}
{title2=$(a)_v,\quad a\in K^\times$}

An <idele> obtained by mapping the same nonzero element of a <number field> into every completion. Only finitely many local valuations are nonzero, so this diagonal tuple belongs to the <idele group>.

= Discreteness of principal ideles
{parent=Principal idele}

The diagonal multiplicative group of a <number field> is discrete in its <idele group>. Require local units at every finite place and an archimedean neighbourhood of one. A diagonal element $x$ meeting these restrictions has integral $x-1$. If $x\ne1$, its nonzero integer <field norm> cannot have absolute value less than one, while sufficiently small archimedean differences would force exactly that.

= Idele-to-ideal homomorphism
{parent=Idele group}
{title2=$(x_v)_v\mapsto\prod_{v<\infty}\mathfrak p_v^{\operatorname{ord}_v(x_v)}$}

The map from an <idele> to its finite-place <fractional ideal> is surjective, has kernel the product of the infinite multiplicative groups and finite-place unit groups, and sends diagonal elements to <principal fractional ideals>. It realizes the <ideal class group> as the corresponding quotient of the <idele group>.

= Idelic modulus
{parent=Idele group}
{title2=$\|x\|=\prod_v|x_v|_v$}

The idelic modulus is the product of normalized local moduli: ordinary real modulus, squared complex modulus and $(N\mathfrak p)^{-\operatorname{ord}_{\mathfrak p}(x)}$ at finite places. This continuous homomorphism to $\mathbb R_{>0}$ has the diagonal multiplicative group in its kernel by the <product formula>. It differs from the <idele norm>, which maps between idele groups of field extensions.

= Norm-one idele group
{parent=Idelic modulus}
{title2=$J_K^1$}

The norm-one idele group is the kernel of the <idelic modulus>. Its quotient by the diagonal $K^\times$ is <compact> for a <number field> and surjects onto the <ideal class group>.

= Idele
{parent=Idele group}
{title2=$(x_v)_v$}

An idele is an element of the <idele group>: a tuple of nonzero elements of the <completion of a valued field> at all places, with finite-place components in the local unit groups except at finitely many places.

= Restricted product topology on the idele group
{parent=Idele group}

The idele topology is the restricted product topology with respect to $\mathcal O_v^\times$. It is also the subspace topology induced by $x\mapsto(x,x^{-1})$ from $\mathbb A_K\times\mathbb A_K$.

= Idèle class group
{title2=$C_K$}
{parent=Idele group}
{wiki}

The idele class group is $C_K=\mathbb I_K/K^\times$, where $K^\times$ is embedded diagonally.

= Idelic congruence subgroup
{title2=$C_K(\mathfrak m)$}
{parent=Idèle class group}

The ideles congruent to one modulo $\mathfrak m$ have finite components in $1+\mathfrak p^{n_\mathfrak p}$ at primes in the finite modulus and positive components at selected real places. Their image in $C_K$ is the idelic congruence subgroup.

= Norm group of an abelian extension
{title2=$N_{L/K}C_L$}
{parent=Idèle class group}

For a finite abelian extension $L/K$, the norm group is the image of the idele-class norm $C_L\to C_K$. Class field theory gives $C_K/N_{L/K}C_L\cong\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$.

= Idele norm
{title2=$N_{L/K}:\mathbb I_L\to\mathbb I_K$}
{parent=Norm group of an abelian extension}

The $v$-component of the idele norm is $\prod_{w\mid v}N_{L_w/K_v}(x_w)$. It descends to an <idèle class group> norm $C_L\to C_K$.

= Nonabelian norm-residue kernel theorem
{parent=Norm group of an abelian extension}

For a finite Galois extension $L/K$ with group $G$, the kernel of the global reciprocity map $C_K\to G^{\mathrm{ab}}$ is $N_{L/K}C_L$. It follows that this norm group equals the norm group from the maximal abelian subextension $L^{[G,G]}/K$.

= Kronecker–Weber theorem
{c}
{parent=Class field theory}
{wiki}

Every finite abelian extension of $\mathbb Q$ is contained in a cyclotomic field.

= Quadratic Kronecker–Weber theorem by ramification
{parent=Kronecker–Weber theorem}
{title2=$\mathbb Q(\sqrt D)\subseteq\mathbb Q(\zeta_{|D|})$}

For an odd prime $\ell$, the unique <quadratic subfield of a prime cyclotomic field> has no finite <ramification> outside $\ell$. The classification of <quadratic discriminants> identifies it with $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{\ell^*})$, where $\ell^*=(-1)^{(\ell-1)/2}\ell$. A fundamental <discriminant> factors as $D=D_2\prod_{\ell\mid D,\ \ell\text{ odd}}\ell^*$ with $D_2\in\{1,-4,8,-8\}$. The remaining <quadratic number field> is generated by $i$, $\sqrt2$, or $\sqrt{-2}$ and lies in $\mathbb Q(\zeta_8)$. Taking the corresponding <compositum> proves the indicated quadratic case of the <Kronecker–Weber theorem>.

= Hilbert class field
{c}
{parent=Class field theory}
{wiki}

The Hilbert class field is the maximal unramified abelian extension of a number field. Its Galois group is canonically the ideal class group.

= Class number divisibility for a CM extension
{parent=Hilbert class field}
{title2=$h_{K^+}\mid h_K$}

For a <CM-field> $K$ over its maximal real subfield $K^+$, the ordinary <Hilbert class field> $H_{K^+}$ is totally real because its real places split. Thus $H_{K^+}\cap K=K^+$. Its <compositum> with $K$ is an abelian extension of $K$ of degree $h_{K^+}$, and is everywhere unramified by <unramified extensions remain unramified under base change>. It lies in $H_K$, giving the divisibility. The argument does not require injectivity of extension of <ideal classes>.

= Hilbert class field of Q of square root minus twenty-three
{c}
{parent=Hilbert class field}
{title2=$H_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-23})}=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-23},\theta),\quad\theta^3-\theta-1=0$}

The three reduced primitive <binary quadratic forms> of discriminant $-23$ are $(1,1,6)$ and $(2,\pm1,3)$, so the <class number> is three. The displayed cubic has <discriminant> $-23$ and is irreducible. Its <splitting field> has <Galois group> $S_3$ and is cyclic cubic over $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-23})$. Modulo $23$ it factors as $(X-10)^2(X-3)$. Lift the simple factor; the remaining quadratic has discriminant valuation one, so its local <inertia group> has order two. Its intersection with $A_3$ is trivial. Thus the relative cyclic cubic extension is everywhere unramified and equals the <Hilbert class field>.

= Hilbert class field of Q of square root 229
{parent=Hilbert class field}
{c}
{title2=$H=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{229},\alpha),\qquad\alpha^3-4\alpha-1=0$}

The polynomial is irreducible with <discriminant> $229$, so its splitting field has <Galois group> $S_3$ and quadratic subfield $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{229})$. The splitting field is cyclic cubic over that subfield. It is unramified outside 229, and at 229 its inertia is a transposition, meeting the alternating cubic subgroup trivially. All roots are real. It is therefore everywhere <unramified> over the quadratic field. The <ideal class group of Q of square root 229> has order three, so this extension is exactly the <Hilbert class field>.

= Hilbert class field of Q of square root minus thirty
{c}
{parent=Hilbert class field}
{title2=$H_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-30})}=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt{-3},\sqrt5)$}

The <ideal class group> of $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-30})$ is $C_2\times C_2$. Its four reduced <binary quadratic forms> have coefficients $(1,0,30)$, $(2,0,15)$, $(3,0,10)$ and $(5,0,6)$. The displayed <multiquadratic field> has degree four over the base. The seven quadratic-subfield discriminants are $8,-3,5,-24,40,-15,-120$, whose product in <absolute value> is $120^4$. The <conductor-discriminant formula> and discriminant tower formula imply that the <relative discriminant> over $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-30})$ is the <unit ideal>. This unramified abelian degree-four extension is therefore its <Hilbert class field>.

= Hilbert class field characterization
{parent=Hilbert class field}

The Hilbert class field is the maximal everywhere-unramified abelian extension of a number field; its Galois group is canonically the ideal class group.

= Class number divisibility with split real places
{parent=Hilbert class field}
{title2=$h_A\mid h_B$}

If a finite extension $B/A$ of <number fields> has a totally ramified finite prime, the ordinary <Hilbert class field> $H_A$ is disjoint from $B$. Its base change $BH_A/B$ is abelian and unramified at finite primes. Its real places also split, since a split-real unramified extension retains this property under base change. It therefore lies in the ordinary <Hilbert class field> of $B$, proving $h_A\mid h_B$ even when $A$ has real places.

= Hilbert class field of Q of square root minus thirty-one
{c}
{parent=Hilbert class field}
{title2=$H_K=K(\theta),\quad K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-31}),\quad\theta^3+\theta-1=0$}

The <splitting field> of $T^3+T-1$ has discriminant square class $-31$ and <Galois group> $S_3$. It is the <Hilbert class field> of $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-31})$: the relative extension is unramified cyclic cubic, and the <ideal class group of Q of square root minus thirty-one> has order three. Locally at $31$, the simple residue root $28$ lifts and the remaining quadratic splits over $\mathbb Q_{31}(\sqrt{-31})$, so the prime of $K$ above $31$ splits completely in its <Hilbert class field>. The inverse generator satisfies $T^3-T^2-1=0$.

= Unramified cyclic cubic extension from a prime-discriminant cubic
{parent=Hilbert class field}
{title2=$\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-p}))\cong C_3$}

Suppose an irreducible monic integral cubic has discriminant $-p$ for a prime $p>3$, and let $L$ be its <splitting field>. Its <Galois group> is $S_3$ and its quadratic subfield is $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-p})$. The extension over that subfield is cyclic cubic and everywhere unramified. Away from $p$, separable reduction and <Hensel lemma> put the roots in an unramified local extension. At $p$, discriminant <valuation> one gives a double residue root and a separate simple root; lift the simple root, and the remaining quadratic's discriminant has square class $-p$. Its splitting field is already the quadratic subfield's completion. The imaginary quadratic base has no real places.

= Class number divisibility under total ramification
{title2=$h_L\mid h_M$}
{parent=Hilbert class field}

If $M/L$ is an extension of <number fields>, $L$ has no real places, and one finite prime of $L$ is totally ramified in $M$, then $h_L\mid h_M$. The <Hilbert class field> $H_L$ has trivial intersection with $M$: any common intermediate extension would be both unramified and totally ramified at that prime. Thus $MH_L/M$ is an unramified abelian extension of degree $h_L$, and is contained in $H_M$.

= Unramified cubic extension of Q of square root minus fifty-nine
{title2=$\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-59})\subseteq\operatorname{Spl}_{\mathbb Q}(T^3+2T+1)$}
{parent=Hilbert class field}

The splitting field has <Galois group> $S_3$ and quadratic subfield $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-59})$. The extension over that subfield is abelian of degree three and unramified everywhere. The only possible rational ramified prime is $59$; at that prime a simple residue root lifts, so the local splitting field is quadratic and coincides with the quadratic subfield's completion. It follows from the <Hilbert class field> degree that $3\mid h_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-59})}$.

= Hilbert class field of Q of square root minus six
{title2=$H_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-6})}=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt{-3})$}
{c}
{parent=Hilbert class field}

The <ideal class group> of $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-6})$ has order two, and
$$
H_K=K(\sqrt2)=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2,\sqrt{-3}).
$$
This quadratic extension of $K$ is unramified at every finite prime and therefore has the degree required of the <Hilbert class field>.

= Dirichlet character
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

A Dirichlet character modulo $m$ is a homomorphism $(\mathbb Z/m\mathbb Z)^\times\to\mathbb C^\times$, extended periodically to integers by zero on numbers not coprime to $m$.

= Real Dirichlet character
{parent=Dirichlet character}

A real Dirichlet character takes values in $\{0,1,-1\}$. Its restriction to the unit group has order at most two; if it is nonprincipal, the order is exactly two. A real nonprincipal <primitive Dirichlet character> is the character to which a possible <Siegel zero> belongs.

// Target: analytic-number-theory.bigb

= Character parity
{parent=Dirichlet character}
{title2=$\chi(-1)=(-1)^a,\quad a\in\{0,1\}$}

The parity determines the gamma factor and theta transformation in the <completed Dirichlet L-function>. It is the sign of the character at minus one.

= Odd Dirichlet character
{parent=Character parity}
{title2=$\chi(-1)=-1$}

A <Dirichlet character> with odd parity satisfies $\chi(-n)=-\chi(n)$, so its extension to <integer> arguments is an <odd function>.

= Even Dirichlet character
{parent=Character parity}
{title2=$\chi(-1)=1$}

A <Dirichlet character> with even parity satisfies $\chi(-n)=\chi(n)$, so its extension to <integer> arguments is an <even function>.

= Conductor of a Dirichlet character
{parent=Dirichlet character}
{title2=$q_\chi$}

= Dirichlet conductor
{c}
{synonym}

The least divisor of a character's modulus through whose unit-group reduction the <Dirichlet character> factors. The <Chinese remainder theorem> reduces its construction to choosing the least exponent at each <prime>. Surjectivity of reduction on units makes the descended <primitive Dirichlet character> unique.

= Character sum of a Dirichlet character
{parent=Dirichlet character}

= Character sums of Dirichlet characters
{synonym}

A character sum of a <Dirichlet character> $\chi$ is a finite <sum> such as $\sum_{n\leq x}\chi(n)$ or the weighted <sum> $\sum_{n\leq x}a_n\chi(n)$. <Orthogonality of Dirichlet characters> converts <residue-class> restrictions to these sums.

= Nonprincipal Dirichlet character
{parent=Dirichlet character}

A <nonprincipal Dirichlet character> differs from the <principal Dirichlet character>. Equivalently, it is a nontrivial character on the unit group, so its sum over one full residue period is zero by <character orthogonality>. Its <Dirichlet L-function> is entire by <Mellin continuation of a nonprincipal Dirichlet L-function>.

= Principal Dirichlet character
{parent=Dirichlet character}
{title2=$\chi_0$}

The <principal Dirichlet character> modulo $N$ is one on integers coprime to $N$ and zero on all other integers. Its <Dirichlet L-function> is $L(\chi_0,s)=\zeta(s)\prod_{p\mid N}(1-p^{-s})$, so it has a simple <pole> at one. For $N>1$ it is induced from the character of modulus one and is not a <primitive Dirichlet character>.

= Gauss sum of a Dirichlet character
{c}
{parent=Dirichlet character}
{title2=$g(\chi)=\sum_{a\bmod N}\chi(a)e^{2\pi ia/N}$}

= Gauss sums of Dirichlet characters
{c}
{synonym}

Extend a <Dirichlet character> by zero on nonunits. Its Gauss sum is $g(\chi)=\sum_{a\bmod N}\chi(a)e^{2\pi ia/N}$, and its finite Fourier transform at $r$ is $S_\chi(r)=\sum_{a\bmod N}\chi(a)e^{2\pi ira/N}$.

= Prime-field character Gauss sum identity
{parent=Gauss sum of a Dirichlet character}
{title2=$|\sum_{y\ne0}\chi(y)e^{2\pi iay/p}|^2=p$}

For a nontrivial <Dirichlet character> modulo a <prime> $p$ and $a\not\equiv0\pmod p$, set $\tau=\sum_{y\ne0}\chi(y)e(ay/p)$. Substituting $y=tz$ in $|\tau|^2$ gives $\sum_{t\ne0}\chi(t)\sum_{z\ne0}e(a(t-1)z/p)$. The inner sum is $p-1$ at $t=1$ and $-1$ otherwise. Since <character orthogonality> gives $\sum_{t\ne0}\chi(t)=0$, the result is $p$.

= Prime-power Gauss sum bound
{parent=Gauss sum of a Dirichlet character}
{title2=$|q^{-1/2}\tau(\chi)|\le1$}

For a character of modulus $p^k$, a <primitive Dirichlet character> attains the bound by finite <Plancherel theorem>. An imprimitive character with $k\ge2$ has periodic values modulo $p^{k-1}$, so its frequency-one sum vanishes. The <principal Dirichlet character> modulo $p$ has unnormalized sum $-1$. The sign convention of the exponential does not affect its magnitude.

= Finite Fourier transform of a primitive Dirichlet character
{parent=Gauss sum of a Dirichlet character}
{title2=$S_\chi(r)=\overline\chi(r)g(\chi)$}

For a <primitive Dirichlet character>, $S_\chi(r)=\overline\chi(r)g(\chi)$ for every integer $r$, with zero on nonunits. For a unit $r$ this follows by substitution. If a prime $p$ divides both $r$ and $N$, primitivity supplies a unit $u\equiv1\pmod{N/p}$ with $\chi(u)\ne1$; substitution by $u$ fixes the additive exponential and forces $S_\chi(r)=0$. For an imprimitive <Dirichlet character> this vanishing can fail.

= Primitive Dirichlet character
{parent=Dirichlet character}

A <Dirichlet character> modulo $N$ is primitive if its character on $(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z)^\times$ does not factor through reduction to the unit group for any proper divisor of $N$. Its least inducing modulus is called its conductor. Primitivity is essential in the usual formula expressing its finite Fourier transform as a conjugate character times a <Gauss sum of a Dirichlet character>.

= Orthogonality of Dirichlet characters
{c}
{parent=Dirichlet character}

For reduced residue classes $a,n$ modulo $q$,
$$
\frac1{\varphi(q)}
\sum_{\chi\bmod q}\overline{\chi(a)}\chi(n)
=
\begin{cases}
1,&n\equiv a\pmod q,\\
0,&n\not\equiv a\pmod q.
\end{cases}
$$
This is the orthogonality relation for the characters of the finite <abelian group> $(\mathbb Z/q\mathbb Z)^\times$.

= Kronecker symbol
{title2=$\left(\frac an\right)$}
{parent=Dirichlet character}
{wiki}

The Kronecker symbol extends the <Jacobi symbol> to all integer denominators. For a fundamental discriminant $D$, the function $\chi_D(n)=\left(\frac Dn\right)$ is the primitive quadratic <Dirichlet character> associated with $\mathbb Q(\sqrt D)$.

= Dirichlet L-function
{title2=$L(s,\chi)$}
{parent=Dirichlet character}
{c}
{wiki}

For $\operatorname{Re}s>1$,
$$
L(s,\chi)=\sum_{n\geq1}\frac{\chi(n)}{n^s}
=\prod_p(1-\chi(p)p^{-s})^{-1}.
$$

= Product over all Dirichlet characters
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}
{title2=$\mathcal P_q(s)=\prod_{\chi\bmod q}L(s,\chi)$}

For real $s>1$, <orthogonality of Dirichlet characters> gives $\log\mathcal P_q(s)=\varphi(q)\sum_{p\nmid q}\sum_{j\ge1,\ p^j\equiv1\pmod q}1/(jp^{js})\ge0$. Thus $\mathcal P_q(s)\ge1$. Only the <principal Dirichlet character> contributes a <pole> at one, and that <pole> is simple. A zero of a nonreal <Dirichlet L-function> at one brings a zero of its conjugate too; their two zeros overpower that <pole> and would make the product tend to zero. This proves nonvanishing for nonreal <Dirichlet characters> and is a useful complement to arguments for <real Dirichlet characters>.

= Global partial-fraction expansion of a Dirichlet L-function logarithmic derivative
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}
{title2=$L'/L=B_\chi-\tfrac12\log(q/\pi)-\tfrac12\Gamma'/\Gamma((s+a)/2)+\sum_\rho((s-\rho)^{-1}+\rho^{-1})$}

For a primitive nonprincipal <Dirichlet character> of conductor $q$ and parity $a$, the <completed Dirichlet L-function> is entire of order one. Its genus-one <Hadamard factorization> gives the displayed <logarithmic derivative>, with $B_\chi$ the logarithmic derivative of the completion at zero and zeros counted with multiplicity. Combined summands converge locally normally away from zeros. For an imprimitive character induced by $\chi^*$, add $\sum_{p\mid Q,\ p\nmid q}\chi^*(p)\log p/(p^s-\chi^*(p))$ for the removed Euler factors. The primitive principal case is the <global partial-fraction expansion of the zeta logarithmic derivative>.

// Target: algebraic-number-theory.bigb

= Trivial zero of a Dirichlet L-function
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}

= Trivial zeros of a Dirichlet L-function
{synonym}

For a primitive nonprincipal <Dirichlet character> with parity $a$, the <gamma function> poles in its <completed Dirichlet L-function> force zeros of $L(s,\chi)$ at $s=-a-2m$, $m=0,1,\ldots$. The functional equation and <nonvanishing of nonprincipal Dirichlet L-functions at one> show that these zeros are simple and cancel the poles, rather than remaining zeros of the completion. For the <Dirichlet beta function>, the odd-parity examples are $\beta(-1)=\beta(-3)=\cdots=0$. The primitive principal conductor-one case is different: its zeta trivial zeros start at $-2$ and the completion has poles at zero and one.

= Nonnegative zeta-times-real-L coefficients
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}
{title2=$a_n=\sum_{d\mid n}\chi(d)\ge0\quad(\chi\text{ real})$}

The coefficients of $\zeta(s)L(s,\chi)$ are multiplicative. On $p^k$, their values are $k+1$ when $\chi(p)=1$, alternating one and zero when $\chi(p)=-1$, and one when $\chi(p)=0$. In particular $a_{m^2}\ge1$. Their logarithmic Euler coefficients $\Lambda(n)(1+\chi(n))$ are also nonnegative, supporting <uniqueness of a possible exceptional real Dirichlet zero>.

= Nonvanishing of Dirichlet L-functions on the line one
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}
{title2=$L(1+it,\chi)\ne0$}

For a nonprincipal <Dirichlet character>, the line is zero-free. <Euler product positivity for L-function nonvanishing> proves this for nonreal characters at every height, and for all characters at nonzero height, where a possibly principal squared-character factor has no <pole>. The point of height zero is the <nonvanishing of nonprincipal Dirichlet L-functions at one>. A principal L-function has a <pole> at one, and no zeros at the other points of this line.

= Imprimitive Dirichlet L-function Euler correction
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}
{title2=$L(s,\chi)=L(s,\chi_0)\prod_{p\mid q,\ p\nmid q_0}(1-\chi_0(p)p^{-s})$}

The inducing <primitive Dirichlet character> has <conductor of a Dirichlet character> $q_0$. Increasing the modulus removes Euler factors only at new <prime> divisors. The correction creates zeros on the imaginary axis; raising the exponents of <primes> already dividing the <conductor of a Dirichlet character> changes no Euler factor. Primitive gamma factors continue to use $q_0$. For real-even characters this includes principal primitive <conductor of a Dirichlet character> one, with its <Riemann zeta function> <pole> treated separately.

= Completed Dirichlet L-function
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}
{title2=$\Lambda(s,\chi)=(q/\pi)^{(s+a)/2}\Gamma((s+a)/2)L(s,\chi)$}

For a primitive nonprincipal <Dirichlet character>, the theta Mellin integral makes this an <entire function> satisfying $\Lambda(s,\chi)=\varepsilon_\chi\Lambda(1-s,\overline\chi)$. The gamma <poles> cancel the <trivial zeros of a Dirichlet L-function>. For the <principal Dirichlet character> whose <conductor of a Dirichlet character> is one the completion is <meromorphic>, with <poles> at zero and one.

= Dirichlet character theta function
{c}
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}
{title2=$\theta_\chi(x)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}n^a\chi(n)e^{-\pi n^2x/q}$}

For a <primitive Dirichlet character> whose <conductor of a Dirichlet character> is $q>1$ and whose <character parity> is $a$, <Poisson summation> gives $\theta_\chi(x)=\varepsilon_\chi x^{-a-1/2}\theta_{\overline\chi}(1/x)$, where $\varepsilon_\chi=\tau(\chi)/(i^a\sqrt q)$. Its rapid decay at both ends yields the entire Mellin representation of the <completed Dirichlet L-function>. The case of <conductor of a Dirichlet character> equal to one uses the ordinary <Jacobi theta function> and a subtracted constant term.

= Character theta transformation
{parent=Dirichlet character theta function}
{title2=$\Theta_\chi(\tau)=\dfrac{g(\chi)}{i^aN^{a+1}(-i\tau)^{a+1/2}}\Theta_{\overline\chi}(-1/(N^2\tau))$}

For a <primitive Dirichlet character> modulo $N>1$ of parity $a\in\{0,1\}$, put $\Theta_\chi(\tau)=\sum_n n^a\chi(n)e^{\pi i n^2\tau}$, extending the character by zero on nonunits. The square root is analytic with positive value when $-i\tau$ is positive real. Apply <Poisson summation> separately in each residue class modulo $N$, and use the <finite Fourier transform of a primitive Dirichlet character>. The <Fourier transforms> of $e^{\pi i\tau x^2}$ and $x e^{\pi i\tau x^2}$ are $(-i\tau)^{-1/2}e^{-\pi i t^2/\tau}$ and $(t/\tau)(-i\tau)^{-1/2}e^{-\pi i t^2/\tau}$. These give the formula, including its odd-character phase.

= Character theta proof of the eta product
{parent=Character theta transformation}
{title2=$\eta(\tau)=\tfrac12\sum_n\chi_{12}(n)q^{n^2/24}$}

Take the even <primitive Dirichlet character> $\chi_{12}$ whose values at $1,5,7,11$ modulo twelve are $1,-1,-1,1$. Its <Gauss sum of a Dirichlet character> is $\sqrt{12}$. The displayed series $A$ has $A(-1/\tau)=\sqrt{-i\tau}A(\tau)$ and $A(\tau+1)=e^{\pi i/12}A(\tau)$. Thus $A^{24}$ is the normalized weight-twelve <modular discriminant>. It has no interior zeros by the <valence formula for the modular group>. Its <logarithmic derivative> equals $E_2/24$, so integration gives the <Dedekind eta function> product. The odd character modulo four similarly produces a series $B$ with $B^8=A^{24}$ and leading term $q^{1/8}$; consequently $B=A^3$.

= Mellin continuation of a nonprincipal Dirichlet L-function
{c}
{parent=Dirichlet L-function}

For a <nonprincipal Dirichlet character> modulo $N$, set
$$
H_\chi(t)=\frac{\sum_{a=1}^N\chi(a)e^{-at}}{1-e^{-Nt}}.
$$
The numerator vanishes at $t=0$, so $H_\chi$ is analytic there and exponentially decreasing as $t\to\infty$. Initially for $\operatorname{Re}s>1$, $\Gamma(s)L(\chi,s)=\int_0^\infty H_\chi(t)t^{s-1}\,dt$. This integral already extends holomorphically to $\operatorname{Re}s>0$. Subtracting finite <Taylor polynomials> near zero, as in <meromorphic continuation of a Mellin transform from an asymptotic expansion>, continues it with possible simple poles only at nonpositive integers. Zeros of the reciprocal <Gamma function> cancel these, proving that $L(\chi,s)$ is an <entire function>. This does not require a <primitive Dirichlet character>.

= Dirichlet density
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A set $S$ of prime ideals has Dirichlet density
$$
\delta(S)=\lim_{s\to1^+}
\frac{\sum_{\mathfrak p\in S}N\mathfrak p^{-s}}
{\log(1/(s-1))},
$$
when the limit exists.

= Chebotarev density theorem
{c}
{parent=Dirichlet density}
{wiki}

For a finite <Galois extension> $L/K$ and a <conjugacy class> $C$ in $G=\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$, the <unramified> primes with <Frobenius conjugacy class> $C$ have <Dirichlet density> $|C|/|G|$. In particular,
$$
\sum_{\mathfrak p:\,\operatorname{Frob}_{\mathfrak p}\in C}(N\mathfrak p)^{-s}
=\frac{|C|}{|G|}\log\frac1{s-1}+O(1)\quad(s\downarrow1).
$$
One proof uses <Brauer induction>, the <Artin L-function order at one>, and <character orthogonality>. The trivial <Artin L-function> is the <Dedekind zeta function> of $K$. The full theorem also gives the natural density, but this logarithmic estimate alone establishes the <Dirichlet density> assertion.

= Character prime sums in the Chebotarev proof
{parent=Chebotarev density theorem}
{title2=$\sum_{\mathfrak p\,\mathrm{unramified}}\chi(g_{\mathfrak p})(N\mathfrak p)^{-s}=\langle\chi,1_G\rangle_G\log(1/(s-1))+O(1)$}

The logarithm from the <Euler product> of an <Artin L-function> has first-prime-power term $\sum\chi(g_{\mathfrak p})(N\mathfrak p)^{-s}$. Higher powers are uniformly bounded as real $s\downarrow1$: $|\chi(g)|\leq\chi(1)$ and $\sum_{\mathfrak p}\sum_{m\geq2}(N\mathfrak p)^{-ms}/m\leq2\sum_{\mathfrak p}(N\mathfrak p)^{-2}<\infty$. The finitely many ramified local factors also have bounded logarithms. The <Artin L-function order at one> therefore proves the displayed estimate. Expanding a conjugacy-class indicator by <character orthogonality> leaves only the trivial character's logarithmic term, with coefficient $|C|/|G|$. This proves <Dirichlet density> directly; a natural-density assertion needs additional analytic arguments.

= Field trace
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

The field trace $\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(x)$ is the trace of multiplication by $x$, equivalently the sum of its conjugates in a separable extension.

= Trace reduction in a ramified local extension
{parent=Field trace}
{title2=$\overline{\operatorname{Tr}_{L/F}(a)}=e\operatorname{Tr}_{\kappa_L/\kappa_F}(\bar a)$}

For an <integral element> of a finite extension of non-Archimedean <local fields>, reduce the multiplication endomorphism on $\mathcal O_L$ modulo a base <uniformizer>. Its filtration by the powers of the maximal ideal has $e$ successive quotients, each the <residue field> $\kappa_L$. Multiplication by $a$ induces multiplication by $\bar a$ on each quotient. Additivity of the <matrix trace> through the filtration proves the formula. If the <residue characteristic> divides the <ramification index>, the trace of every integral element lies in the base maximal ideal; a <Galois extension> is not required.

= Integral trace need not imply integrality
{parent=Field trace}
{title2=$\operatorname{Tr}(x)\in\mathcal O_K\ \not\Longrightarrow\ x\in\mathcal O_L$}

In $L=\mathbb Q_p(\theta)$ with $\theta^2=p$, the <Eisenstein polynomial> $X^2-p$ gives $v_L(\theta)=1$ and $v_L(p)=2$. The element $x=\theta/p$ has <field trace> zero, but <valuation> $-1$, so is not in the <valuation ring>. Cancellation in a <field trace> contrasts with the multiplicative test in <unit norm detects units in a local extension>.

= Trace of an integral element over a normal domain
{parent=Field trace}

For a <normal domain> $A$ with <fraction field> $K$, the <field trace> of an <integral element> in a finite <separable field extension> $L/K$ belongs to $A$. Its conjugates are integral, so their sum is integral; it lies in $K$ and hence in $A$ by normality. This places the <integral closure> inside a <trace-dual lattice>.

= Trace pairing
{parent=Field trace}

For a finite field extension $L/K$, the trace pairing is the <bilinear form>
$$
(x,y)\longmapsto\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(xy).
$$
It is nondegenerate exactly when $L/K$ is separable.

= Coefficient pairing for a monogenic algebra
{parent=Trace pairing}
{title2=$\lambda(ab)=[X^{n-1}](ab\bmod g)$}

For a monic degree-$n$ <polynomial> $g$ over a <commutative ring> $R$, let $A=R[X]/(g)$ and let $\lambda$ take the coefficient of $X^{n-1}$ in the reduced representative. The pairing $(a,b)\mapsto\lambda(ab)$ is unimodular on $1,X,\ldots,X^{n-1}$: reversing one basis order gives a triangular matrix with diagonal ones. When $A$ embeds in a separable field extension, <Lagrange interpolation> identifies $\lambda(a)$ with $\operatorname{Tr}(a/g'(x))$. This yields the derivative formula for the <inverse different>.

= Trace-dual lattice
{parent=Trace pairing}

For an $R$-lattice $M$ in $L$, its trace-dual lattice is
$$
M^\vee=\{x\in L:\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(xM)\subseteq R\}.
$$
For the integral closure $S$ of $R$, the trace dual $S^\vee$ is the <inverse different>.

= Trace dual of a fractional ideal
{title2=$\mathfrak b^\vee=\mathfrak D_{K/\mathbb Q}^{-1}\mathfrak b^{-1}$}
{parent=Trace-dual lattice}

For a nonzero <fractional ideal> $\mathfrak b$ of a <number field>, its <trace-dual lattice> is the <fractional ideal>
$$
\mathfrak b^\vee=\{z\in K:\operatorname{Tr}_{K/\mathbb Q}(z\mathfrak b)\subseteq\mathbb Z\}=\mathfrak D_{K/\mathbb Q}^{-1}\mathfrak b^{-1}.
$$
The equality follows from the definition of the <inverse different> and invertibility of <fractional ideals>. Under the positive metric on the <Minkowski embedding of a number field>, the Euclidean <dual lattice> of $j(\mathfrak b)$ is $\overline{j(\mathfrak b^\vee)}$: coordinatewise <complex conjugation> converts the positive <inner product> into the <trace pairing>. This assertion does not require that conjugation preserve the number field itself.

= Discriminant ideal
{title2=$\operatorname{disc}(S/R)$}
{parent=Trace pairing}

For the integral closure $S$ of a <Dedekind domain> $R$ in a finite separable extension, the discriminant ideal is locally generated by
$$
\det\bigl(\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(\alpha_i\alpha_j)\bigr),
$$
where $(\alpha_i)$ is a local $R$-basis of $S$. Intrinsically it is the image of $(\det_R S)^{\otimes2}$ under the determinant of the <trace pairing>.

= Relative discriminant
{title2=$\mathfrak d_{L/K}$}
{parent=Discriminant ideal}

The relative discriminant $\mathfrak d_{L/K}$ of a finite extension of <number fields> is the <discriminant ideal> of $\mathcal O_L$ over $\mathcal O_K$. It satisfies
$$
|d_L|=|d_K|^{[L:K]}N_{K/\mathbb Q}(\mathfrak d_{L/K}),
$$
and its prime divisors are exactly the ramified finite primes.

= Norm of the different ideal
{title2=$\mathfrak d_{E/K}=N_{E/K}(\mathfrak D_{E/K})$}
{parent=Relative discriminant}

The <relative discriminant> of a finite extension of <number fields> is the relative <norm of a fractional ideal> applied to its <different ideal>. Consequently, at $\mathfrak p$,
$$
v_{\mathfrak p}(\mathfrak d_{E/K})=\sum_{\mathfrak P\mid\mathfrak p}f_{\mathfrak P/\mathfrak p}d_{\mathfrak P},
$$
where $f_{\mathfrak P/\mathfrak p}$ is the <residue-field degree> and $d_{\mathfrak P}$ is the <different exponent>. Over $\mathbb Q$, $N(\mathfrak D_{E/\mathbb Q})=|D_E|$.

= Trace form of a number field
{title2=$B(x,y)=\operatorname{Tr}_{K/\mathbb Q}(xy)$}
{parent=Field trace}

The trace form of a number field $K$ is the symmetric bilinear form
$$
B(x,y)=\operatorname{Tr}_{K/\mathbb Q}(xy).
$$

The pairing uses the <field trace> of the product, rather than taking a trace of each factor separately.

= Nondegeneracy of the trace form of a number field
{parent=Trace form of a number field}

For nonzero $x\in K$, choose $y=x^{-1}$. Then
$$
B(x,y)=\operatorname{Tr}_{K/\mathbb Q}(1)=[K:\mathbb Q]\ne0,
$$
so the trace form is <nondegenerate bilinear form>[nondegenerate].

= Trace and norm integrality criterion for a quadratic extension
{parent=Field trace}

In a quadratic extension $L/K$, an element is integral exactly when its relative trace and norm lie in $\mathcal O_K$.

= Ideal norm
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

For a nonzero integral ideal $I\subseteq\mathcal O_K$, its norm is the finite index $N(I)=|\mathcal O_K/I|$. Norms multiply under ideal multiplication.

= Conjugate product of a quadratic ideal
{parent=Ideal norm}
{title2=$\mathfrak a\bar{\mathfrak a}=(N(\mathfrak a))$}

For a nonzero integral ideal $\mathfrak a$ of the full <ring of integers of a number field> of degree two,
$$
\mathfrak a\overline{\mathfrak a}=(N(\mathfrak a)).
$$
Here conjugation is the nonidentity field automorphism and $N(\mathfrak a)$ is the additive index of the ideal. A direct proof writes $\mathfrak a=s\langle d,e+\omega\rangle_{\mathbb Z}$ in an <integral basis> $1,\omega$. Put $\theta=e+\omega$, $T=\theta+\bar\theta$, $U=\theta\bar\theta$. The ideal property gives $d\mid U$. Moreover $\gcd(d,T,U/d)=1$: a prime dividing all three would make $\theta/\ell$ an <algebraic integer>, contradicting its nonintegral coefficient of $\omega$. The product of $\langle d,\theta\rangle$ with its conjugate contains $d^2,dT,U$, so <Bezout identity> puts $d$ in it; all its generators lie in $(d)$. Scaling back gives $(s^2d)$, and the lattice determinant identifies $s^2d=N(\mathfrak a)$. The full ring of integers is essential to the integrality argument.

= Norm of a fractional ideal
{title2=$N_{L/K}(I)$}
{parent=Ideal norm}

For a fractional ideal $I$ of $\mathcal O_L$, the relative ideal norm is the fractional ideal of $\mathcal O_K$ obtained multiplicatively from prime-ideal norms. Equivalently, its local valuation is the determinant valuation of $I$ as an $\mathcal O_K$-lattice relative to $\mathcal O_L$.

= Relative ideal norm from norms of elements
{parent=Norm of a fractional ideal}
{title2=$N_{L/K}(I)=\langle N_{L/K}(x):x\in I\rangle$}

For a nonzero <fractional ideal> of a <number field>, the displayed generated ideal equals the relative <norm of a fractional ideal>. Locally, multiplication by $x$ on the integral lattice has determinant $N_{L/K}(x)$. A <Smith normal form> calculation and the residue filtrations give $v_{\mathfrak p}(N(x))=\sum_{\mathfrak P\mid\mathfrak p}f_{\mathfrak P/\mathfrak p}v_{\mathfrak P}(x)$. The <Chinese remainder theorem for ideals> chooses $x\in I$ simultaneously attaining the minimum valuation of $I$ at each prime above a fixed $\mathfrak p$. Thus the generated ideal has valuation $\sum f_{\mathfrak P/\mathfrak p}v_{\mathfrak P}(I)$. Adding these valuations proves multiplicativity, including <fractional ideals> after clearing denominators.

= Ideal class group
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{wiki}

The ideal class group is the group of nonzero fractional ideals modulo principal fractional ideals.

= Ideal class group of Q of square root 229
{parent=Ideal class group}
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{229}))\cong C_3$}

Put $\omega=(1+\sqrt{229})/2$, $P=(3,\omega)$ and $Q=(5,\omega-4)$. The <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> is $\sqrt{229}/2<8$, so the <ideal class group> is generated by primes above three and five; two and seven are inert. The <prime ideal factorizations> $(6+\omega)=PQ$ and $(15+2\omega)=P^3$ reduce it to a cyclic group of order dividing three. It is not trivial: the unit $\epsilon=(15+\sqrt{229})/2$ has <field norm> minus one. If an algebraic integer had norm of absolute value three, multiplying by a power of this unit would make both real absolute values at most $\sqrt{3\epsilon}$. For $a=x+y\omega$, this bounds $|y|\sqrt{229}=|a-a'|\leq2\sqrt{3\epsilon}<\sqrt{229}$, forcing $y=0$, which cannot have norm of absolute value three.

= Ideal class group of the imaginary quadratic field of discriminant minus eighty-four
{parent=Ideal class group}
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-21}))\cong C_2\times C_2$}

For $s=\sqrt{-21}$, the integer ring is $\mathbb Z[s]$. The <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> is less than six. The ramified primes $(2,1+s)$ and $(3,s)$ give independent classes of order two; their product is the class of $(5,s-2)$ because $(3+s)$ factors as the product of these three prime ideals. Element norms $a^2+21b^2$ cannot equal 2, 3 or 6, so these classes are distinct. Ideals of norm at most five supply no further classes, proving the four-element group.

= No integer solutions of the Mordell equation with constant minus twenty-one
{parent=Ideal class group of the imaginary quadratic field of discriminant minus eighty-four}
{title2=$y^2=x^3-21$}

Parity and valuations at 3 and 7 make the ideals $(y+\sqrt{-21})$ and $(y-\sqrt{-21})$ coprime in any hypothetical solution. Their product is a cube, so the first is an ideal cube. The <ideal class group> has exponent two, forcing its cube root ideal to be principal. Cubing $a+b\sqrt{-21}$ would then give coefficient $3b(a^2-7b^2)$ on $\sqrt{-21}$, which cannot equal one. This proves the nonexistence of integer solutions without assuming unique element factorization.

= Narrow ideal class group
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}^{+}(K)$}
{parent=Ideal class group}

= Narrow class group
{synonym}

The narrow ideal class group of a <number field> is the group of nonzero <fractional ideals> modulo <principal fractional ideals> generated by <totally positive> elements. It is the <ray class group> for the modulus consisting of all real <Archimedean places> and trivial finite part. The forgetful map to the ordinary <ideal class group> is surjective, with <kernel> $\{\pm1\}^{r_1}/\operatorname{sgn}(\mathcal O_K^\times)$ under the <unit signature map>.

= Ideal class group of Q of square root minus thirty-one
{parent=Ideal class group}
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-31}))\cong C_3$}

The <ideal-form correspondence for imaginary quadratic fields> and the reduction bound $a\leq\sqrt{31/3}$ give exactly the forms $[1,1,8]$, $[2,1,4]$ and $[2,-1,4]$. Thus the <class number> is three and the ideal class group is cyclic of order three.

= Ideal-form correspondence for imaginary quadratic fields
{parent=Ideal class group}
{title2=$[a,b,c]\longleftrightarrow(a,(-b+\sqrt D)/2)$}

For a negative fundamental discriminant $D$, primitive positive definite integral <binary quadratic forms> of discriminant $D$, modulo proper equivalence, correspond bijectively to the <ideal class group> of $\mathbb Q(\sqrt D)$. The displayed ideal gives one conventional choice of this correspondence. <Reduced positive definite binary quadratic forms> give unique class representatives with the usual boundary sign convention, making class-number computations finite.

= Ideal class group of Q square root of minus fourteen
{parent=Ideal class group}
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-14}))\cong C_4$}

In $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-14})$, the <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> is less than five. The <ideals> $\mathfrak p=(2,\sqrt{-14})$ and $\mathfrak q=(3,1+\sqrt{-14})$ generate all classes, with $\mathfrak p^2=(2)$ and $(2-\sqrt{-14})=\mathfrak p\mathfrak q^2$. No element has <field norm> two, so $[\mathfrak p]$ has order two and $[\mathfrak q]$ has order four.

= Ideal class
{parent=Ideal class group}

An ideal class is an element of the <ideal class group>, represented by a nonzero <fractional ideal>. Two fractional ideals represent the same class exactly when their quotient is a <principal ideal>.

= Class number
{title2=$h_K$}
{parent=Ideal class group}
{wiki}

The class number of a number field is the order $h_K=|\operatorname{Cl}(K)|$ of its <ideal class group>.

= Minkowski's bound
{parent=Ideal class group}
{c}
{wiki}

= Minkowski bound for ideal classes
{c}
{synonym}

If a number field of degree $n$ and signature $(r_1,r_2)$ has discriminant $d_K$, every ideal class contains an integral ideal $I$ with
$$
N(I)\leq
\left(\frac4\pi\right)^{r_2}
\frac{n!}{n^n}\sqrt{|d_K|}.
$$

= Class number one for Q of cube root six
{parent=Minkowski's bound}
{title2=$h_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt[3]{6})}=1$}

For $\alpha^3=6$, the <ring of integers> is $\mathbb Z[\alpha]$ and its <field discriminant> is $-972$. The <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> is $16\sqrt3/\pi<9$. Prime ideals of norm at most eight lie above $2,3,5,7$. The primes above two, three and the degree-one prime above five are generated by $\alpha-2$, $3+2\alpha+\alpha^2$ and $\alpha-1$, of norm magnitudes $2,3,5$. At seven the residue roots are $3,5,6$; the primes at roots six and three are generated by $\alpha+1$ and $\alpha^2+\alpha-5$, both of norm seven. Their product with the remaining prime is $(7)$, making that prime principal too. Every possible Minkowski representative is therefore principal.

= Minkowski lower bound for a number-field discriminant
{c}
{parent=Minkowski's bound}

Since every nonzero integral ideal has norm at least one, the <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> implies
$$
|d_K|\geq
\left(\frac\pi4\right)^{2r_2}
\left(\frac{n^n}{n!}\right)^2
$$
for every number field of degree $n$ and signature $(r_1,r_2)$.

= Class group of Q of cube root of three
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt[3]{3}))$}
{parent=Minkowski's bound}
{c}

For $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt[3]{3})$ with $\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[\sqrt[3]{3}]$, the field discriminant is $-243$ and the Minkowski bound is $8\sqrt3/\pi<5$. The prime ideals above $2$ and $3$ are principal, so every ideal class has a principal representative and
$$
\operatorname{Cl}(K)=1.
$$

= Ideal class group of Q of square root of minus one hundred sixty-three
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-163}))$}
{parent=Minkowski's bound}
{c}

The imaginary quadratic field $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-163})$ has discriminant $-163$, and its <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> is
$$
\frac2\pi\sqrt{163}<9.
$$
The polynomial $X^2-X+41$ defining its <ring of integers of a quadratic field> has no root modulo $2,3,5,$ or $7$, so these rational primes are inert. Factoring an integral ideal of norm at most eight into <prime ideals> then shows that its only possible nontrivial factor is the principal ideal $(2)$, of norm four. Every ideal class is therefore principal, and
$$
\operatorname{Cl}(K)=1.
$$

= Ideal class group of Q of square root of minus thirty-three
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-33}))$}
{parent=Minkowski's bound}
{c}

The field has ring of integers $\mathbb Z[\sqrt{-33}]$, discriminant $-132$, and Minkowski bound below eight. The nonprincipal ramified ideals above two and three generate four classes, and the split primes above seven introduce no new class. Hence
$$
\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-33}))
\cong C_2\times C_2.
$$

= Ideal class group of Q of square root of minus twenty-two
{title2=$\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-22}))\cong C_2$}
{parent=Minkowski's bound}
{c}

The ring of integers is $\mathbb Z[\sqrt{-22}]$, its discriminant is $-88$, and its <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> is $4\sqrt{22}/\pi<6$. The primes three and five are inert, while
$$
(2)=\mathfrak p_2^2,
\qquad
\mathfrak p_2=(2,\sqrt{-22}).
$$
The ideal $\mathfrak p_2$ is nonprincipal because $a^2+22b^2=2$ has no integer solution. Every class is represented by an ideal of norm at most five, so $[\mathfrak p_2]$ is the only possible nontrivial class and the class group is $C_2$.

= No integral solutions to y cubed equals x squared plus twenty-two
{title2=$y^3=x^2+22$}
{parent=Ideal class group of Q of square root of minus twenty-two}

The equation $y^3=x^2+22$ has no integer solutions. Factoring it in $\mathbb Z[\sqrt{-22}]$, the two conjugate factors are coprime. <Unique factorization of ideals in a number field> and the absence of three-torsion in the class group force $x+\sqrt{-22}$ to be a cube up to a unit. Writing it as $(a+b\sqrt{-22})^3$ makes its $\sqrt{-22}$ coefficient
$$
b(3a^2-22b^2)=1,
$$
which has no integer solution.

= Euler prime-generating quadratic from class number one
{parent=Ideal class group}
{c}

Let $m\geq2$, let $p=4m-1$ be prime, and suppose $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-p})$ has class number one. If $n\geq0$ and $n^2+n+m<m^2$, then $n^2+n+m$ is prime. The key identity is the <algebraic norm>
$$
n^2+n+m=N\left(n+\frac{1+\sqrt{-p}}2\right).
$$
A hypothetical prime divisor below $m$ would split and yield an algebraic integer of that norm, but the positive norm form $(a^2+pb^2)/4$ represents no prime below $m$.

= Order of an ideal class
{parent=Ideal class group}

The order of the class of a fractional ideal $I$ is the least positive integer $n$ for which $I^n$ is principal.

= Odd-order obstruction for a split prime in an imaginary quadratic field
{parent=Order of an ideal class}

Let $p\equiv11\pmod{12}$, $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-p})$, and let $\mathfrak p$ be a prime-ideal factor of $(3)$. If $p>3^{n+2}$ and $n$ is odd, then $[\mathfrak p]$ does not have order $n$. Otherwise $\mathfrak p^n=(\alpha)$ would give
$$
4\cdot3^n=a^2+pb^2
$$
for $\alpha=(a+b\sqrt{-p})/2$. The bound forces $b=0$, after which $3^n=N(\alpha)$ is an integer square, impossible for odd $n$.

= Bounded-norm ideal representatives
{parent=Ideal class group}

If every integral ideal $I$ contains nonzero $\alpha$ with $|N(\alpha)|\leq C N(I)$, then $(\alpha)I^{-1}$ is an integral representative of $[I]^{-1}$ with norm at most $C$.

= Finiteness of the ideal class group
{parent=Ideal class group}

Bounded-norm representatives prove class-group finiteness because only finitely many integral ideals have norm at most a fixed constant.

= Ideal classes are torsion
{parent=Ideal class group}

Every element of a finite ideal class group has finite order. Therefore every nonzero ideal has a positive power that is principal.

= Unique factorization of ideals in a number field
{parent=Algebraic number theory}

Every nonzero proper ideal of a number ring factors uniquely as a finite product of positive powers of distinct nonzero prime ideals.

= Dedekind factorization theorem
{parent=Unique factorization of ideals in a number field}
{c}

If $\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[\theta]$ and the minimal polynomial of $\theta$ factors modulo a rational prime $p$ as
$$
\overline f=\prod_i\overline g_i^{e_i},
$$
then
$$
(p)=\prod_i(p,g_i(\theta))^{e_i},
$$
and the prime ideal $(p,g_i(\theta))$ has norm $p^{\deg g_i}$.

This gives an explicit instance of <unique factorization of ideals in a number field>.

= Monogenic field
{parent=Dedekind factorization theorem}
{wiki}

= Monogenic number field
{synonym}

A number field $K$ is monogenic when its ring of integers has a power integral basis, meaning $\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[\theta]$ for some algebraic integer $\theta$.

= Completely split small-prime obstruction to monogenicity
{parent=Monogenic field}

Let $K$ have degree $n$, and suppose a rational prime $p$ factors into $n$ distinct prime ideals of norm $p$. If $n>p$, then $K$ is not monogenic. Otherwise the <Dedekind factorization theorem> would force the degree-$n$ minimal polynomial of a power-basis generator to have $n$ distinct linear factors over $\mathbb F_p$, although only the $p$ polynomials $X-a$ are available.

= Dedekind zeta function
{parent=Algebraic number theory}
{c}
{wiki}

For a number field $K$, the Dedekind zeta function is formally
$$
\zeta_K(s)=\sum_{0\ne\mathfrak a\subseteq\mathcal O_K}N(\mathfrak a)^{-s}.
$$

= Dedekind zeta function as a permutation Artin L-function
{parent=Dedekind zeta function}
{title2=$\zeta_K(s)=L_{\mathbb Q}(\mathbb C[\operatorname{Hom}(K,E)],s)$}

Let $E$ be a finite normal closure of the <number field> $K$. The <permutation representation> of its <Galois group> on the embeddings of $K$ into $E$ has one trivial constituent, because the action is transitive. At a prime, the <inertia group> invariant basis consists of sums over inertia orbits of embeddings; <Frobenius automorphism> permutes them in cycles of lengths equal to the <residue degrees> of the primes of $K$. A cycle of length $f$ contributes $\det(1-T\rho(\operatorname{Frob}))=1-T^f$. Hence the local factors equal those of the <Dedekind zeta function>, including at ramified primes. If nontrivial irreducible <Artin L-functions> are holomorphic and nonzero at one, this factorization and the simple pole of the <Riemann zeta function> show that $\zeta_K$ has a simple pole there.

= Euler product for the Dedekind zeta function
{parent=Dedekind zeta function}

Unique factorization of nonzero ideals and multiplicativity of the ideal norm give the formal identity
$$
\zeta_K(s)=\prod_{\mathfrak p}
\left(1-N(\mathfrak p)^{-s}\right)^{-1},
$$
where the product ranges over nonzero prime ideals.

= Quadratic Dedekind zeta factorization
{parent=Dedekind zeta function}

For a quadratic field $K$ of discriminant $D$,
$$
\zeta_K(s)=\zeta_{\mathbb Q}(s)L(\chi_D,s),
$$
where $\chi_D(p)$ is $1$, $-1$, or $0$ according as $(p)$ splits, remains prime, or ramifies in $\mathcal O_K$. Comparing the three possible local Euler factors proves the formal identity.

= Analytic class number formula
{parent=Dedekind zeta function}
{wiki}

For a number field of signature $(r_1,r_2)$, roots-of-unity count $w_K$, regulator $R_K$, class number $h_K$, and discriminant $d_K$,
$$
\operatorname*{Res}_{s=1}\zeta_K(s)
=\frac{2^{r_1}(2\pi)^{r_2}h_KR_K}{w_K\sqrt{|d_K|}}.
$$

= Quadratic class number formula
{parent=Analytic class number formula}

For an imaginary quadratic field of fundamental discriminant $D<-4$,
$$
h(D)=\frac{\sqrt{|D|}}{\pi}L(1,\chi_D).
$$
Equivalent finite character-sum forms follow by evaluating the odd quadratic <Dirichlet L-function> at one.
